// ── 3. THE PITCH ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("3. The Card Pitch"));
body.push(para("The pitch is how the dealer delivers cards to each player. It is arguably the most skill-dependent physical task in dealing and the one that separates professional dealers from amateurs most visibly. A poor pitch results in exposed cards, misdeals, player complaints, and slowed game pace. An excellent pitch is fast, flat, accurate, and completely conceals every card until the player looks at it."));

body.push(h2("3.1 Grip and Mechanics"));
body.push(para("The pitch is taught in the TruePokerDealer curriculum as the skill that benefits most from learning correctly the first time. Mark Shumer emphasizes: it is extremely important to learn the pitch correctly from the start, because once the body trains muscle memory for an incorrect pitch, unlearning it is an enormous effort. Dealers who learn wrong develop chronic injuries and have to essentially start over."));
body.push(h3("Body Position"));
body.push(para("Elbows must be in by the sides, not flared out. Palms face the ceiling (or tilted very slightly inward). The deck hand (non-dominant, typically left) holds the deck with a slight angle, tilted approximately 15 to 20 degrees from horizontal. This angle allows the thumb to push the top card off smoothly. The deck must NOT be held flat (makes card removal harder) and must NOT be tilted toward players. A deck tilted toward any player exposes card faces on every single pitch. Players who can see the flash will not say anything because they benefit from the information, so the dealer may never know they are exposing cards."));
body.push(h3("Deck Hand Grip"));
body.push(para("The forefinger rests on the front edge of the deck, protecting the front card from sliding forward and exposing. However, the forefinger must not press too high: if it rides up on the card face, every card pushed off the deck will bend upward against the finger and flash its value. The three remaining fingers (middle, ring, pinky) rest along the side of the deck, feeling for how many cards are being pushed off (must always be exactly one). The thumb is positioned on top of the deck in a flexible position, ready to push cards off."));
body.push(h3("The Primary Pitch (Top Corner Grab)"));
body.push(para("Step 1: The thumb pushes the top card slightly off the deck toward the upper-right corner. Do not push the card too far off. Step 2: The throwing hand grabs the card at the top-right corner with the thumb underneath and the index finger on top. The grip is loose, not tight. If the thumb and index finger squeeze too hard, the card will bend upward during projection and flash. Step 3: The middle finger finds its \u2018divot\u2019 \u2014 a comfortable grip point along the card edge that is slightly different for every dealer. This middle finger is what propels the card. Step 4: The card is projected by opening the hand. The middle finger extends outward, pushing the card, while the thumb and index finger release simultaneously. The follow-through is the hand opening fully with all fingers extended."));
body.push(para("Critical: There is NO wrist flick and NO arm motion. The entire pitch comes from opening the hand. This is not a suggestion for better form \u2014 it is a medical necessity. Dealers who pitch with wrist action develop ganglion cysts (a fluid-filled bump on the wrist caused by repetitive strain). Multiple dealers in the TruePokerDealer community have reported developing ganglion cysts specifically from incorrect pitch mechanics, with the cysts persisting for years. Dealers who pitch with arm motion are slower, less accurate, and fatigue faster."));
body.push(h3("The Secondary Pitch (Center Grab)"));
body.push(para("An alternative grip for situations where the primary pitch is insufficient. The card is pushed further off the deck and grabbed toward the center of the card (slightly above center) rather than the top corner. The middle finger goes to the back of the card. This grip allows the card to project more easily and travel further, which is useful when dealing on tables with poor felt (where cards do not glide well) or when the dealer needs extra distance. The center grab is slower than the top corner grab, which is why it is taught as a secondary technique. Professional dealers learn both grips and switch between them as table conditions require."));
body.push(h3("Directional Pitching and Chair Position"));
body.push(para("To pitch to different positions around the table, the dealer turns their entire body in the chair (a swivel chair is essential). The dealer should always try to be as centered as possible toward the target position. Pitching straight ahead is the most accurate trajectory. Pitching sideways (reaching to the far left or right without turning) causes cards to fly off-angle, increases exposure risk, and accelerates shoulder strain. In extreme cases where a player is at the very edge of the table, the dealer may need to angle their body rather than relying on the arm alone."));
body.push(h3("Keeping Cards Face-Down During Pitch"));
body.push(para("The most common pitch failure is a card flipping face-up during delivery. The primary cause is the wrist being angled downward rather than upward. As noted in TruePokerDealer training: to keep cards down when pitching, turn the wrist up toward the ceiling more. This simple adjustment changes the card\u2019s trajectory from a tumbling arc to a flat glide. Dealers who consistently flip cards have their wrist position corrected first before any other adjustment is attempted."));
body.push(boldPara("Common Pitch Errors (observed in real casino environments): ", "Pitching with wrist action (causes ganglion cysts and chronic wrist injuries). Holding the deck tilted toward players (flashes every card dealt). Gripping the card too tightly with thumb and index finger (causes it to bend upward and flash). Using arm motion instead of finger extension (slower, less accurate, more fatiguing). Not turning the body in the chair when pitching to edge positions (causes inaccuracy and shoulder strain). Forefinger riding too high on the front of the deck (bends every card pushed off)."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must deliver individual cards from a held deck to specific positions around a semicircular table (up to 10 player positions). Cards must travel flat (no flutter or flip) and land face-down at each player position. Card faces must not be visible at any point during the pitch. System must be capable of delivering 2 cards to each of 10 positions (20 pitches) in under 15 seconds for competitive speed. Each card must land within approximately 6 inches of the target position. The pitching mechanism must be able to vary trajectory angle to reach all table positions without moving the entire base unit."));

body.push(h2("3.2 Pitch Situations and Edge Cases"));
body.push(h3("Exposed Cards During Pitch"));
body.push(para("If a card flips face-up during the pitch (due to poor technique, table obstruction, or player interference), the rules vary by casino. In most cardrooms, if the exposed card is the first card dealt to a player, it becomes the burn card for the first betting round and the player receives a replacement. If it happens on the second card, the exposed card is shown to the entire table, set aside, and becomes the first burn card. The dealer must announce any exposure immediately."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "System must detect (via computer vision) if any card flips or exposes during delivery. Must have error-recovery protocol that identifies the exposed card, announces it to the table, removes it from the hand, and follows the applicable replacement procedure. This requires real-time card identification capability."));

body.push(h3("Card Leaves the Table"));
body.push(para("If a pitched card flies off the table entirely, it is treated as an exposed card. The dealer calls the floor (supervisor) if there is any dispute. The card is typically killed and the player receives a replacement after the deal is complete."));

body.push(h3("Card Lands on Another Player\u2019s Cards"));
body.push(para("If a card lands on or near another player\u2019s hole cards, the dealer must ensure neither player can see the other\u2019s cards. This is a \u201Cone player per hand\u201D integrity issue. The floor may be called if there is any suspicion of information leakage."));

// ── 4. BRINGING COMMUNITY CARDS ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("4. Community Cards: Flop, Turn, and River"));
body.push(para("After the preflop betting round is complete, the dealer must bring the community cards in a specific sequence. This is one of the most rule-dense procedures in dealing and one of the highest-risk moments for error."));

body.push(h2("4.1 The Pre-Flop to Flop Transition"));
body.push(para("The transition from preflop to the flop is one of the most procedurally dense moments in dealing. As taught in TruePokerDealer Lesson 18, the exact sequence is:"));
body.push(para("Step 1: Bring all bets in. After all preflop action is complete, the dealer sweeps all bets into the center pot. The pot should be gathered neatly."));
body.push(para("Step 2: Tap the table. This is a gentle tap, not a slap or a hammer. At the World Series of Poker in particular, loud taps are actively discouraged. The tap serves as an audible notification that the flop is coming. This is critical because it is possible that a player has not acted and nobody at the table realized it. The most common case: the big blind is last to act preflop, often only needs to check, and sometimes the dealer or table assumes they have checked when they have not. The tap gives that player a final moment to act before community cards are exposed."));
body.push(para("Step 3: Burn a card. The burn card procedure requires precise hand mechanics to prevent any exposure. The dealer pushes the top card off the deck with the thumb of the deck hand. Then the throwing hand takes the card with the index finger underneath and the thumb on top. The dealer opens their full hand against the card, creating a shield. The deck is brought close to the felt, and the burn card is pulled down underneath the hand so that it is completely covered throughout the entire motion from deck to felt. The burn card goes underneath the pot or in a diagonal pattern toward the dealer."));
body.push(boldPara("Critical detail: ", "Many inexperienced dealers grab the burn card with just the thumb and finger (like a pinch grip) and pull it down in a way that briefly exposes the corner. Players positioned to the dealer\u2019s left or right can see this flash. Those players will not say anything because seeing the burn card is free information for them. The correct technique involves opening the full hand over the card as a complete shield while bringing it to the felt. The card must be invisible from the moment it leaves the deck to the moment it is on the felt. A robotic system must ensure zero exposure of the burn card during removal."));
body.push(para("Step 4: Deal three flop cards. For each card, the dealer places the index finger underneath the card and the thumb on top, then brings it down to the felt face-down. All three cards are dealt this way, roughly aligned in a row. The dealer then slides their thumb along one edge and the other fingers along the opposite edge to push the three cards neatly together. This creates a single block of three cards."));
body.push(para("Step 5: Spread the flop. The dealer places their thumb on the edge of the three-card block and pulls/slides the cards apart so there is visible felt between each card. This is the display position. Every card must be clearly visible to all player positions. Cards that are touching or overlapping are a sign of an inexperienced dealer and make it harder for players to read the board."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must deal 3 cards face-down, align them into a block, then spread them face-up with visible felt separation between each card. Cards must be readable from all player positions simultaneously. The spread spacing and orientation must be consistent every time. The burn card must be completely shielded during removal \u2014 zero exposure tolerance."));

body.push(h2("4.2 Turn and River"));
body.push(para("The turn and river each follow the same pattern: tap the table (gently), burn one card (fully shielded using the same hand-over-card technique), deal one card face-down, then flip it face-up. For the flip: the dealer pushes the card out from the deck, turns their hand upside down so the index finger is on top and the thumb is underneath on the top corner, then flips the card face-up. The dealer may flip it directly into position next to the existing community cards, or flip it in front of themselves and then slide it into position. Both methods are accepted; the choice is often a matter of dealer preference or supervisor instruction."));
body.push(para("The turn card goes to the right of the flop. The river card goes to the right of the turn. Burn cards are placed in a diagonal pattern angled toward the dealer, away from the community cards. This diagonal placement serves a critical purpose: if a player accidentally mucks a card onto the burn pile area, the diagonal orientation makes it easy to distinguish burn cards from mucked cards. This protects the hand in case of any dispute about which cards belong where."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must execute the burn-and-turn sequence 3 times per hand (flop, turn, river) with zero card exposure on burns. Must maintain a clean, consistent community card layout that is visible from all 10 player positions simultaneously. Burn cards must be physically separated from community cards in a distinguishable pattern."));

// ── 5. BETTING ROUND MANAGEMENT ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("5. Betting Round Management"));
body.push(para("Managing the betting rounds is the most cognitively demanding task a poker dealer performs. It requires simultaneously tracking who has acted, how much each player has bet, whether any bets are outstanding, making change, and managing the pot. This is where the majority of dealer errors occur and where robotic precision would deliver the highest value."));

body.push(h2("5.1 Following the Action"));
body.push(para("The dealer must always know whose turn it is to act. Preflop, action starts with the player to the left of the big blind (under the gun) and proceeds clockwise. On all subsequent streets, action starts with the first remaining player to the left of the dealer button. The dealer must track verbal declarations (check, bet, call, raise, fold, all-in) and physical actions (placing chips forward, pushing cards toward the muck, sliding their entire stack forward). When verbal and physical actions conflict, the verbal declaration takes priority in most cardrooms."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must track the action state of every player (has not acted, has acted, has folded, is all-in) in real time. Must process both verbal commands (via speech recognition) and physical chip/card movements (via computer vision). Must resolve conflicts between verbal and physical actions according to house rules. Must prompt the correct player when it is their turn and detect out-of-turn action."));

body.push(h2("5.2 Bets, Calls, and Making Change"));
body.push(para("Players place their bets in front of them, not directly into the pot (splashing the pot is prohibited because it makes it impossible to verify the bet amount). The dealer must verify each bet amount, ensure raises meet the minimum (typically double the previous bet in no-limit), and make change when a player puts out a chip larger than their intended bet. For example, if the bet is $15 and a player puts out a single $25 chip, the dealer must determine if this is a call (change of $10 back) or a raise. Under the oversized chip rule, a single chip without a verbal declaration is a call."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must identify chip denominations by color and/or RFID. Must count bet amounts in real time. Must apply the oversized chip rule and minimum raise rules automatically. Must calculate and deliver correct change. Must detect and reject invalid bet sizes (under-calls, insufficient raises) and prompt the player for correction."));

body.push(h2("5.3 Pot Management"));
body.push(para("After each betting round is complete (all active players have put in equal amounts or are all-in), the dealer sweeps all bets into the center pot. This must be done cleanly with one hand pushing chips inward while the other guards the pot. The pot should be a neat stack, not a scattered pile, though it does not need to be counted unless a player requests a count (in pot-limit games, the dealer must be able to state the pot size at any time)."));

body.push(h2("5.4 Straddles"));
body.push(para("A straddle is a voluntary blind bet, typically double the big blind, placed before any cards are dealt. Straddles are cash game only \u2014 they do not exist in tournaments. The straddle functions as a third blind: the action starts to the left of the straddle, and the straddler acts last preflop (just like the big blind in an unstraddled hand). The dealer must announce \u2018live straddle\u2019 loudly and clearly every time, and often needs to announce it twice, because players trained in normal action flow will attempt to act from the wrong position."));
body.push(para("Timing rule: the official rule in most rooms is that the straddle must be posted before the first card is off the deck. In practice, many dealers allow it before the straddling player receives their first card. The stricter rule is safer for auditions and new dealers. Once announced or posted, the straddle is binding \u2014 the player cannot take it back once dealing has begun."));
body.push(h3("Straddle Positions and Variations"));
body.push(para("Under-the-gun (UTG) straddle: the most common and simplest form. The player immediately to the left of the big blind posts double the big blind. Action starts to their left. Some rooms only allow UTG straddles. Button straddle: allowed in many rooms. The player on the button posts double the big blind. Action starts on the small blind. The dealer must be vigilant because the UTG player will instinctively try to act first \u2014 this is the most common error with button straddles. Mississippi straddle (any position): some rooms allow straddling from any seat. If two players want to straddle, the player in the best position (last to act post-flop) gets priority. The dealer must announce where the action starts and actively prevent players from acting out of order."));
body.push(para("Consecutive (re-)straddles: some games (typically higher stakes or private games) allow sequential straddles where each player can re-straddle for double the previous straddle. This can cascade around the table: $10, $20, $40, $80, $160. The lock-in rules vary by room \u2014 some lock the straddle once first card is dealt, others once posted. Sleeper straddle: a straddle from a middle position that only activates if all players between the blinds and the sleeper fold. If any player in between puts money in, the sleeper comes back. Not common in casinos but seen in some rooms."));
body.push(h3("Straddle Effects on Game Mechanics"));
body.push(para("In no-limit: the straddle becomes the effective big blind for minimum bet/raise purposes. A 2/5 game with a $10 straddle has a minimum raise of $20 preflop, and the minimum post-flop bet becomes $10 (the straddle amount, not the big blind). In limit: the straddle counts as the bet (not a raise), so you still get three raises on top of it \u2014 the cap goes one increment higher than normal. Example: 4/8 limit with a full kill straddle to $8 means cap is $8-$16-$24-$32."));
body.push(para("Live vs. dead straddle: a live straddle gives the player last action preflop (they can raise if unraised). A dead straddle does not \u2014 it is just a blind raise with no special position. Dead straddles are largely obsolete but may exist in some limit games. Straddle-in: in rooms that allow straddles from any position, a player who missed their blinds can sometimes straddle in instead of posting a big blind plus dead small. This is considered good for the game because it encourages action."));
body.push(para("Denomination optimization: in 1/2 NL, the original straddle of $4 (double the big blind) was changed to $5 in most rooms because $4 bets and calls create constant change-making that slows the game. The $5 straddle uses the standard red chip denomination. Similarly, 1/3 NL games often use a $5 straddle. This is a small procedural detail that has a large impact on dealing speed."));
body.push(para("Mandatory straddles: some high-stakes and private games have mandatory button straddles or mandatory UTG straddles. The straddle is not optional \u2014 every hand has it. This effectively makes the game play at double the blind level."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must support configurable straddle rules: UTG-only, UTG-and-button, any-position (Mississippi), and consecutive re-straddles. Must correctly adjust the action start position based on straddle location. Must announce the straddle clearly (audio or visual display). Must adjust minimum bet/raise calculations for straddle pots. Must enforce timing rules (straddle before first card dealt). Must support mandatory straddle configurations. Must handle straddle-in as an alternative to missed blind posting where configured."));

body.push(h2("5.5 Kill Pots"));
body.push(para("A kill pot is a hand where the betting limits are increased because a predetermined trigger condition was met. Kills are primarily a limit game feature \u2014 they add excitement and bigger pots to limit formats. They are much less common in no-limit because straddles serve the same purpose. A kill can be a full kill (double the limits) or a half kill (1.5x the limits). Example: a 4/8 limit game with a full kill becomes 8/16 on kill hands; with a half kill it becomes 6/12."));
body.push(h3("Kill Triggers"));
body.push(para("Texas Hold\u2019em kill: triggered when a player wins two consecutive pots where the second pot exceeds a threshold amount (e.g., $40 in a 4/8 game). The first win gives the player a \u2018leg up\u2019 (tracked with a kill button showing \u2018leg up\u2019 on one side and \u2018kill\u2019 on the other). If the same player wins the next pot above the threshold, the kill is activated. Omaha Hi-Lo kill: triggered when a player scoops (wins both high and low) a pot above a certain amount. There is no leg-up system \u2014 a single scoop above the threshold triggers the kill on the next hand. If the pot is split in any way, no kill. The dealer must track pot sizes to know when the threshold is crossed."));
body.push(h3("Kill Pot Mechanics"));
body.push(para("When a kill is triggered, the winning player receives a kill button (turned to the \u2018kill\u2019 side). On the next hand, they post the kill amount (double the big blind for full kill, 1.5x for half kill) regardless of their seat position. The blinds remain the same. Action handling varies by room: some start action to the left of the kill (like a straddle), others start in the normal position with the kill amount as the effective bet. In limit, the kill changes the betting increments for the entire hand: a 4/8 full kill hand uses 8/16 betting. The cap calculation adjusts accordingly. The kill stays active as long as the kill player keeps winning \u2014 it resets when someone else wins a pot."));
body.push(para("Kill obligations: the player with the kill button cannot escape paying the kill. Even if they want to cash out, they must post the kill for that hand first. This is not optional \u2014 it is part of agreeing to play in a kill game."));
body.push(h3("The Rock"));
body.push(para("A rock is a special object (often chips wrapped in a rubber band) used in some games to create a permanent straddle. The player who wins a pot receives the rock. On the next hand, regardless of their position, they must post a straddle using the rock chips. This means every single hand has a straddle. The rock effectively doubles the game \u2014 a 5/10 game with a rock plays like a 10/20 game. The rock is popular in home games and some casino games. Practical note: a rubber-banded rock drags on the felt and causes wear; some games use a single chip or a dedicated marker instead."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must support configurable kill rules: full kill, half kill, per-game triggers (consecutive wins for Hold\u2019em, scoop threshold for Hi-Lo). Must track the kill button state (leg up vs. active kill). Must adjust betting limits dynamically for kill hands. Must track pot sizes for scoop-threshold kills in Hi-Lo. Must support rock games with automatic straddle assignment to pot winners. Kill pots are an excellent showcase for robotic dealing because the limit adjustments, pot tracking, and button management happen seamlessly with no dealer confusion."));

body.push(h2("5.6 Button Movement and Missed Blinds"));
body.push(para("The dealer button determines playing position and blind posting obligations. Button movement is a cash-game-only complexity \u2014 in tournaments, every seat is dealt in every hand and there are no missed blinds. The core rule: a player may never receive the button unless they have paid both the big blind and the small blind. This rule ensures no player can gain the most advantageous position (last to act post-flop) without first having paid the cost of the worst positions."));
body.push(h3("Standard Button Movement"));
body.push(para("The button follows the small blind: after each hand, the button moves to the player who was just the small blind. Under normal circumstances with all seats occupied, this means the button advances one seat clockwise each hand. The cycle is: button \u2192 small blind \u2192 big blind \u2192 under the gun, repeating around the table."));
body.push(h3("Dead Button vs. Active Button Systems"));
body.push(para("Two competing systems exist for handling empty seats. Dead button system: the button moves one seat clockwise every hand, even if that seat is empty. If the button lands on an empty seat, there is simply no small blind for that hand (just a big blind). This system is simpler but creates situations where a new player at the table must wait a hand because the button is in front of their empty seat, and one player may get last position two hands in a row. Active button system (preferred for cash): the button always stays in front of an active player. When a seat empties, the button stays with the closest active player rather than advancing to an empty seat. This system creates more flexibility for seating new players because there is always an active player on the button, but it can result in a player having the button for two consecutive hands (which would have happened anyway under the dead button system). Dead button is preferred for tournaments because it avoids giving any transferring player the button without having paid blinds. Active button is preferred for cash because it maximizes the number of hands dealt and lets new players sit in sooner."));
body.push(h3("Missed Blind Procedures"));
body.push(para("When a player leaves the table and misses their big blind, they receive a missed blind button (a physical marker placed at their seat). When they return, they must post a big blind plus a dead small blind to rejoin the game from any position (the dead small goes into the pot but does not count toward their bet). Exception: if the player returns when it is their natural big blind position, they simply post the big blind with no dead small. If a player only missed a small blind: they must post a dead small to rejoin. However, if they come back on their natural big blind, just the big blind with no dead small is sufficient."));
body.push(para("If a player misses the small blind and subsequent hands pass until they miss the big blind too, the small blind button is replaced with a big blind button \u2014 it is treated as if they only missed the big blind. The big blind miss always takes precedence. A missed small blind does NOT expire into a missed big blind on its own; the transition happens when the big blind position passes the absent player."));
body.push(para("Key rules: (1) A player can NEVER start on the small blind \u2014 they have not paid the big blind. (2) A player can NEVER start on the button \u2014 they have not paid both blinds. (3) Posting a blind or straddle is not considered action for any purpose other than getting dealt in. (4) New players to a table: in rooms that require posting, a new player must post a big blind before being dealt in (many players wait for their natural big blind to avoid the extra post). In rooms that do not require posting, the player is simply dealt in immediately for free hands until the big blind reaches them."));
body.push(h3("Buying the Button"));
body.push(para("Buying the button allows a player to pay both blinds (big blind live + dead small blind) on the current hand so that they receive the button on the next hand. This exists to let players play immediately rather than waiting for the button to pass. The rule for when buying the button is allowed: there must be a small blind to the player\u2019s left and a button to their right. If there is a big blind to their left (e.g., because the natural small blind seat is empty), buying the button does NOT work \u2014 the next hand would leave the buyer without having paid a small blind, breaking the system. When buying the button: the existing small blind and big blind push their blinds back, the buyer posts a big blind and a dead small blind, and the existing blind players receive free action for that hand (the former small blind becomes first to act under the gun). Next hand: the button goes to the buyer, and the former blind players resume their normal small blind/big blind positions."));
body.push(para("If two new players sit down simultaneously adjacent to each other, only the one in worse position (closer to the blinds) is asked first about buying the button. If they buy it, the second player must wait one hand. If they decline, the second player is asked. Both cannot buy in the same hand."));
body.push(h3("Heads-Up Button Rules"));
body.push(para("When the table gets down to two players (heads-up), special rules apply. The button posts the small blind and acts first preflop but last on all subsequent streets. The first card is dealt to the big blind (non-button player), second card to the button. This is the opposite of the normal dealing order. When transitioning from three-handed to heads-up: determine which player was supposed to be the big blind next \u2014 that player remains the big blind. The button goes to the other player. This may mean a player gets the button twice in a row, which is unavoidable."));
body.push(h3("Absent Player Management"));
body.push(para("Poker rooms track how long players are away from the table. Common systems: (1) Digital tracking via player management software (Bravo, PokerAtlas) that flags players as absent after a set time. (2) Physical button tracking: each new dealer that taps in gives the absent player another miss-blind button or an \u2018out\u2019/\u2018absent\u2019 button; two or three absent buttons means the player is picked up (their chips are racked and the seat is opened). (3) Third/fourth man walking rules: when three or more players are absent from a table, the third or fourth player may be picked up immediately to maintain table size. (4) Meal buttons: some rooms allow extended absence once per session for meals."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must implement configurable button movement logic (dead button for tournaments, active button for cash). Must track missed blind status for every seat with automatic button assignment. Must enforce the rule that no player receives the button without paying both blinds. Must support buying the button with correct blind restructuring. Must correctly handle heads-up transitions and multi-way eliminations. Must track player presence and absence duration with configurable pickup thresholds. Button movement logic is a complex state machine that human dealers frequently get wrong, especially in edge cases involving multiple empty seats \u2014 a robotic system eliminates these errors entirely."));

// ── 6. SIDE POTS AND ALL-INS ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("6. Side Pots and Multi-Way All-Ins"));
body.push(para("This is the single most complex procedural challenge in poker dealing and the scenario that causes the most dealer errors in live casino environments. It is also the scenario that would be most dramatically improved by robotic precision, because the math is deterministic but the physical execution under pressure is where human dealers fail. Side pots are the most requested topic in dealer training, the most heavily tested skill in casino auditions, and the single biggest reason auditioners reject otherwise competent dealers. If there is one thing a dealer must get absolutely right before walking into an audition, it is this: make sure the money is always right."));

body.push(h2("6.1 The Core Principle"));
body.push(para("A player can only win from each opponent an amount equal to what they have put in. This is the fundamental rule that creates side pots. Throughout the course of a hand, every player puts chips into the pot in equal amounts relative to each other \u2014 that one-to-one ratio is what entitles them to compete for the money in the pot. When a player\u2019s final chips go into the pot (they are \u2018all-in\u2019), that is the maximum they can win from each other player. The rule exists so that a player who does not have as much money as their opponents is not forced to forfeit the entire hand and lose everything they have already put in simply because they cannot match a larger bet."));
body.push(para("If Player A goes all-in for $200 and Player B calls with a $800 stack and Player C calls with a $1,500 stack, Player A cannot win more than $200 from each opponent. The excess chips that Player A cannot compete for are separated into a side pot that only Player B and Player C can contest."));

body.push(h2("6.2 Procedure: Three-Way All-In with Unequal Stacks"));
body.push(para("Scenario: Player A has $200, Player B has $800, Player C has $1,500. All three go all-in preflop. Assume $50 already in the pot from blinds."));
body.push(h3("Step 1: Build the Main Pot (Starting with the Smallest Stack)"));
body.push(para("Always start with the shortest stack. Identify the player with the least chips \u2014 in this case Player A with $200. Take $200 from each of the three players. Combined with the $50 in blinds, the main pot is $650. All three players are eligible to win this pot. The main pot is always the pot where every single player has put in an equal amount."));
body.push(h3("Step 2: Build Side Pot 1"));
body.push(para("Player B has $600 remaining after the main pot. Player C has $1,300 remaining. Take $600 from each. Side Pot 1 is $1,200. Only Player B and Player C are eligible to win this pot. Player A is dead to it \u2014 none of the money in this side pot is available to them."));
body.push(h3("Step 3: Return Excess"));
body.push(para("Player C has $700 remaining that no one can match. This is returned to Player C immediately."));
body.push(h3("Step 4: Physical Separation"));
body.push(para("The dealer must physically separate the main pot and side pot on the table so they are visually distinct. The main pot is typically placed in the center. Side pots are placed to the side. There is no single designated spot for each pot, but the dealer must ensure: (1) no pot is placed where the community board cards go, (2) no pot is close enough to a player\u2019s stack to be confused with their chips, and (3) pots cannot bounce across or intermingle with each other. In Omaha Hi-Lo games where pots must be stacked for splitting, a stacked pot that falls over and intermingles with a side pot creates a serious problem \u2014 this is one reason experienced dealers prefer to keep pots spread rather than stacked."));
body.push(h3("Step 5: All-In Buttons"));
body.push(para("The all-in button has become a standard tool in casino poker rooms over the last decade, recommended by the Tournament Directors Association (TDA Recommended Procedure 1). The dealer keeps one or two all-in buttons in their chip tray. When a player goes all-in, the dealer pulls the button from the tray and places it clearly in front of the player\u2019s betting area, then verbally announces \u2018all-in\u2019 to the table. The verbal announcement is mandatory; the button does not replace it. The button says ALL-IN on one side and CALL on the other."));
body.push(para("Placement rules: The button must be fully visible to all players. It must NOT be placed underneath chips, on top of chips, or in any position where it could be obscured. Placing the button on top of a player\u2019s chips is considered unprofessional and can create unintentional tells about the dealer\u2019s perception of the situation. The button should not interact with the player\u2019s chips at all."));
body.push(para("Multiple all-ins: If a second player goes all-in, the dealer uses a second button if available. If only one button exists, the dealer slides the button from the first all-in player to the most recent all-in player (the most recent all-in is the most relevant to the current action). If a third player goes all-in, the first button can be moved to them."));
body.push(para("The CALL side: In tournament play, the call button is especially valuable. When a player calls an all-in in a tournament, it is crucial information for all other players at the table because it affects their tournament equity calculations. In cash games, the call button is used less frequently because the stakes are contained to that hand. This is a judgment call for the dealer: in tournaments, always use the call side; in cash games, use judgment."));
body.push(para("Player confirmation: The all-in button alone is not sufficient to confirm a player is all-in. Best practice is to have the player push at least one chip into the pot in addition to the button placement. This prevents situations where a miscommunication results in a player being committed all-in when they did not intend it. Some poker rooms have this as an official or unofficial rule."));
body.push(h3("Step 6: Showdown"));
body.push(para("At showdown, the dealer resolves the LAST (outermost) side pot first, then works backward to the main pot. If Player C has the best hand, they win Side Pot 1 ($1,200) and the Main Pot ($650). If Player A has the best hand overall, they win only the Main Pot ($650), and the side pot is awarded to whichever of Player B or C has the better hand between them."));

body.push(h2("6.3 Two Methods for Building Pots: Cherry Picking vs. Doing the Math"));
body.push(para("There are two fundamental methods for building side pots, and a competent dealer must understand both. The choice of method depends on the situation, and knowing when to use which one is a mark of an experienced dealer."));
body.push(h3("Method 1: Cherry Picking"));
body.push(para("Cherry picking means physically taking the short stack\u2019s amount from every player\u2019s bet, one at a time, and assembling it into the main pot. You identify the smallest all-in amount, then go around the table taking exactly that amount from each player\u2019s chips and placing it into the center as the main pot. The remainder from each player stays out and becomes the side pot. This is the safest, most error-resistant method. If you cherry pick every single time, you will always get it right."));
body.push(para("Example: Five players are in the hand. One player goes all-in for $15 on the flop. Four other players each have $60 bet. The dealer takes $15 from each of the five players \u2014 that is $75 going to the main pot. The remaining $45 from each of the four active players goes to the side pot."));
body.push(para("The downside of cherry picking is speed. If you have to take $15 from every single one of nine players, auditioners may view this as slow and mechanical. In an audition specifically, taking 15 from every stack when the math is simple (15 \u00D7 9 = 135) will make you look like a beginner. However, in a live game, cherry picking is perfectly acceptable and often clearer for the players to follow."));
body.push(h3("Method 2: Doing the Math"));
body.push(para("Doing the math means mentally calculating the total for each pot rather than physically counting it from each player. You identify the short stack amount, multiply it by the number of players, announce that total as the main pot, sweep the appropriate chips in, and declare the rest as the side pot. This is faster and what auditioners generally expect to see."));
body.push(para("Example: Five players, one all-in for $15. Instead of taking $15 from each player individually, the dealer says \u2018$15 from each player, that\u2019s $75 going to the main, the rest is on the outside.\u2019 Then the dealer sweeps the appropriate amounts in one efficient motion. The key communication pattern is: announce the short stack amount, announce the total, declare what goes where. Do it clearly. Do it once. Move on."));
body.push(para("When to use which method: If the math is simple (small number of players, round numbers, easy multiplication), do the math \u2014 it is faster and looks more professional. If the math is complex (odd amounts, many players, multiple streets of accumulated bets), cherry pick \u2014 accuracy always beats speed. A dealer who does the math incorrectly and has to re-sort pots has lost far more time than one who cherry-picked carefully from the start. The critical rule: take your time and make sure you get everything right. Do not feel the pressure players put on you to speed up. If you rush, you will have no idea who is eligible to win what, and that is the worst possible floor call a supervisor could have to resolve."));

body.push(h2("6.4 Multi-Way All-In Procedure: Four or More Players"));
body.push(para("When four or more players are all-in with different stack sizes, the procedure scales but the principle stays the same: always start with the smallest stack, build the main pot first, then work outward building successive side pots."));
body.push(h3("Pre-Pot Building: Verify All Call Amounts"));
body.push(para("Before building any pots, the dealer\u2019s first action is to count down the all-in amount and verify that all calls are correct. Start with the initial all-in bet and confirm each caller has the right amount out. Do not start creating pots until every call amount is verified. If you start building side pots before confirming the bet, and the actual bet differs from what you assumed, you will have to tear everything apart and start over."));
body.push(h3("Building Pots Sequentially"));
body.push(para("For a four-way all-in: Identify the shortest stack. Take that amount from all four players \u2014 this is the main pot (all four eligible). Find the next shortest stack among the remaining three. Take the difference from those three players \u2014 this is Side Pot 1 (three players eligible). Take the difference between the third and second shortest from the remaining two players \u2014 this is Side Pot 2 (two players eligible). Return any unmatched excess to the largest stack."));
body.push(para("For five or more players, the same pattern continues. Each successive side pot includes one fewer player. With nine players all-in at different amounts, you would build up to eight side pots. This is the most extreme scenario a dealer can face \u2014 it can take 15 to 45 minutes to resolve, and the next dealer will be standing behind you waiting to tap in. It is what it is."));
body.push(h3("Pot Tracking with Chip Markers"));
body.push(para("When there are many side pots, the dealer needs a system to remember which player is eligible for which pot. The standard technique is to use chip markers \u2014 take a chip from the tray and angle it toward the player who is all-in for that pot. Use a stacking system: one chip pointing at a player means it is the first (main) pot they are eligible for, two stacked chips means the second pot, three means the third, and so on. This is the chip trail method."));
body.push(para("When building many pots, bring each pot\u2019s chips near the player whose all-in created that pot. Go around the table in one consistent direction each time you cherry-pick. Announce each amount as you take it: \u2018$645 from every player\u2019 \u2014 say the number each time you take from a new stack. This repetition is not redundant; it is what gives the players confidence that you know what you are doing."));
body.push(h3("Space Management"));
body.push(para("A critical practical concern with multi-way all-ins is table space. Before building pots, push player stacks back if needed to create workspace. Always leave room for any remaining community cards (the river, or turn and river). If the board needs to be moved to create space, it can be relocated as long as the cards stay intact and in order. Never place a pot where the board cards go. Never place pots where they could intermingle with player stacks."));
body.push(h3("Hands Must Stay in Front of Players"));
body.push(para("During multi-way all-in situations, all face-up hands MUST remain in front of the player who bet them. This is one of the most important rules a dealer can follow in these scenarios. Never gather hands up to the center of the table or near the board \u2014 this is a practice that originated from televised poker and is one of the worst habits a casino dealer can develop. Leave all hands face-up in front of their respective players. It is much easier and more professional to evaluate hands, push pots, and kill losing hands when every hand is in its natural position."));

body.push(h2("6.5 All-In for Less During Active Betting"));
body.push(para("One of the trickiest scenarios is when a player goes all-in for less than the current bet during an active betting round where other players still have chips to bet. This requires the dealer to build pots mid-street while keeping the action flowing."));
body.push(h3("Scenario: All-In on the Flop with Continuing Action"));
body.push(para("Four players see the flop. Player A bets $55. Player B calls $55. Player C calls $55. Player D goes all-in for $15 (less than the bet). The dealer cherry-picks $15 from every player \u2014 that is $60 to the main pot. The remaining $40 from Players A, B, and C goes to the side pot. Player D is all-in for the main pot only. Subsequent betting on the turn and river among Players A, B, and C all goes into the side pot."));
body.push(para("The key communication: \u2018This player is all-in for the main. The rest is on the side, and we still have active players.\u2019 Then after building the pots, if there is still action to come, announce: \u2018Any additional action is another side pot.\u2019 This prevents confusion when the turn and river bets need their own pot allocation."));
body.push(h3("Multiple All-Ins Across Multiple Streets"));
body.push(para("The complexity increases when different players go all-in on different streets. The first all-in creates the initial main pot and side pot split. If a second player goes all-in on the turn for less than the side pot bet, a second side pot is created from the first side pot. The dealer must track three pots: the main pot (all players), Side Pot 1 (fewer players), and Side Pot 2 (fewest players). Each successive street of betting among the remaining active players flows into the outermost (most recent) side pot."));
body.push(para("Example: On the flop, Player D goes all-in for $15 \u2014 main pot is built. On the turn, Player A bets $115, Player B goes all-in for $80 (less than the bet), Player C calls $115. The dealer now takes $80 from each of the three remaining players for Side Pot 1. The extra $35 each from Player A and Player C creates Side Pot 2. The river betting between Player A and Player C flows into Side Pot 2. At showdown, resolve Side Pot 2 first (between A and C), then Side Pot 1 (between A, B, and C), then the main pot (all four players)."));

body.push(h2("6.6 The Inside-Out Shortcut Method"));
body.push(para("The standard method of building all pots before running the board is the safe, audition-approved approach. But experienced dealers in cash games sometimes use an alternative: the inside-out method, where pots are resolved starting from the winner rather than the smallest stack. This method is faster when the largest stack wins, but it requires confidence and experience. A warning: if you are not an experienced dealer who already knows the standard method flawlessly, do not attempt this. It puts you at risk of making mistakes, and a supervisor may write you up if it goes wrong."));
body.push(h3("How It Works"));
body.push(para("When all players are already all-in and hands are face-up (typically on the river or when all action is complete), instead of building every pot first and then awarding them, you: (1) Identify the best hand. (2) Count that player\u2019s stack. (3) Take that amount from every other player. (4) Push it all to the winner along with the main pot and any pre-existing pots they are eligible for. (5) Kill the losing hands that are now eliminated. (6) Among the remaining players, find the next best hand and repeat."));
body.push(para("The advantage: if the player with the largest stack has the best hand, you take their amount from everyone, push them the entire table\u2019s chips, and you are done in one pass. You have avoided building seven or eight side pots that all go to the same player anyway. This saves enormous time."));
body.push(para("The disadvantage: if the player with the smallest stack has the best hand, you still have to do the same amount of work as the standard method. And if you make an error in identifying the best hand or miscounting, the consequences are harder to unwind because you have already pushed chips."));
body.push(h3("When to Use It"));
body.push(para("The inside-out method works best when: (1) All action is already complete and all hands are face-up \u2014 you know who wins before building pots. (2) The hand that wins has a large stack, meaning you can clear most of the table in one pass. (3) You are an experienced dealer who can frame hands quickly and accurately. It is not appropriate in tournaments where supervisors typically require all pots to be built first, and it is never appropriate in an audition."));

body.push(h2("6.7 Showdown Resolution Order"));
body.push(para("At showdown in a multi-way all-in, the dealer resolves pots from the outside in \u2014 the last (outermost) side pot is resolved first, then the next side pot, working backward to the main pot. This is the standard procedure and the one auditioners expect."));
body.push(para("The dealer says: \u2018These two hands first, for the side pot.\u2019 The two eligible players show (or one shows and the other mucks). The dealer evaluates, kills the losing hand, and pushes the side pot. Then the dealer introduces the next player\u2019s hand for the next pot inward. This continues until the main pot is awarded."));
body.push(para("In cash games, players are not required to show their hands at showdown \u2014 if one player shows, others may muck. But the dealer must still resolve in order: outside pot first, then work inward. Players who are all-in will typically expose their hands, but the dealer should announce which pot is being contested and which hands are relevant before players flip cards."));
body.push(para("Critical rule for showdown: Always frame the board before evaluating hands. Verify the winning hand against the community cards. When killing a losing hand, tap the cards on the table first (confirms the action to surveillance), then place them in the muck. Push the pot, then move to the next pot. Check your rake spot before pushing the final (main) pot \u2014 this is the moment dealers most commonly forget the rake because there is so much happening."));

body.push(h2("6.8 Rake Management During Side Pot Scenarios"));
body.push(para("One of the most common dealer errors during multi-way all-ins is forgetting to take the rake. With so much action, so many pots, and so much pressure, the rake falls out of the dealer\u2019s mental workflow. This is why experienced dealers develop the habit of checking the rake drop slot before pushing the final pot \u2014 it is a trained reflex, not a conscious decision."));
body.push(para("In percentage-rake games, the rake is taken from the pot as it is built during the hand, typically when the flop is dealt and the pot crosses the rake threshold. In multi-way all-in scenarios where the pot grows rapidly, the dealer should take the rake early \u2014 ideally before beginning to build side pots. Once you are deep into sorting eight side pots, you will not remember whether you took the rake, and at that point the pot is distributed across too many separate piles to cleanly extract it."));
body.push(para("If you realize you forgot the rake after pushing the pot, the general rule is: it is too late. You should inform a floor supervisor and let them decide. However, in an extreme multi-way all-in where a player has won many times their investment, asking for the house\u2019s five dollars after they just won thousands is generally accepted and the player will comply. In a time-rake game (common for larger stakes), the rake is collected on a schedule regardless of pot size, so this concern does not apply to the individual hand."));
body.push(para("Making change for the rake from side pots: If you need to extract the rake and the main pot does not have convenient denominations, you can make change from a side pot. Take the amount you need from one pot, replace it with equivalent chips from another. Be transparent and announce what you are doing. Players will accept this as long as you communicate clearly."));

body.push(h2("6.9 Limit Hold\u2019em Side Pots"));
body.push(para("In limit poker, side pots arise more gradually than in no-limit because the betting increments are fixed and smaller. Players tend to get all-in over the course of several streets rather than in one dramatic shove. This creates a unique challenge: the dealer must build side pots incrementally during the flow of active betting, not in one concentrated burst."));
body.push(para("In a 4/8 limit game, for example, a player who started the hand with $8 may be all-in by the flop after calling a pre-flop raise and one bet on the flop. The dealer takes that player\u2019s amount from each other player\u2019s flop bet, builds the main pot, and then continues dealing the hand with the remaining players\u2019 action going to the side pot. Turn and river bets all flow into the side pot."));
body.push(para("The complication in limit: because betting continues in fixed increments, the dealer must continuously track which pot receives which bets. On each street, verify: Is the all-in player\u2019s contribution already accounted for in the main pot? Are all subsequent bets going to the correct side pot? With multiple all-ins across multiple streets in limit (which happens more often than in no-limit because players buy in for smaller amounts relative to the bet sizes), the dealer can easily end up with three or four pots by the river, each built incrementally from different streets."));
body.push(para("Rake tracking in limit multi-way pots is especially tricky. With a percentage rake, the pot may cross multiple rake thresholds as bets are added across streets. Count the number of players on the flop and announce it \u2014 this makes it dramatically easier to calculate the pot size for rake purposes. If you know it is four players and the bet was $4, you know $16 came in and can track the cumulative total. Without that count, you will lose track."));
body.push(h3("All-In for Less Than the Blind"));
body.push(para("In tournaments, a player can go all-in for less than the big blind. They are only eligible to win that amount from each other player. In cash games, house rules vary: some rooms require a minimum of one big blind to play a hand, while others allow any legitimate chip amount. In tournaments specifically, if the blinds are 200/400 with a 400 big blind ante and a player has exactly 400 chips, the ante is posted first. If they win, they only get the antes back. If they had 500, the 400 goes to the ante and only 100 goes toward their big blind \u2014 they can only win 100 from each player\u2019s bet in addition to the antes."));

body.push(h2("6.10 Table Communication and Player Confidence"));
body.push(para("Side pots and multi-way all-ins are the scenario where dealer communication skills matter most. Players are not comfortable in these situations because they have seen dealers make mistakes with their money. The dealer\u2019s job is to earn trust through clear, deliberate communication. Every action should be announced before it is taken."));
body.push(h3("The Communication Pattern"));
body.push(para("Talk your game continuously during pot building. The pattern is: (1) Announce the short stack amount: \u2018This player is all-in for $645.\u2019 (2) Announce the action: \u2018I\u2019m taking $645 from every stack.\u2019 (3) Say the amount each time you take from a player: \u2018$645\u2026 $645\u2026 $645\u2026\u2019 (4) Announce the result: \u2018This is the main pot. This player is eligible.\u2019 (5) Announce the next action: \u2018Now building the side pot.\u2019 This constant narration is not for the dealer\u2019s benefit \u2014 it is for the players. It gives them confidence that the dealer knows exactly what they are doing."));
body.push(h3("Handling Skeptical or Aggressive Players"));
body.push(para("Players who challenge the dealer during side pot construction are not being malicious \u2014 they are worried. They have seen dealers mess up these situations with their money on the line. The effective response is empathy, not authority. Never say \u2018there\u2019s one dealer at the table\u2019 or anything that asserts dominance. Instead, acknowledge their concern: \u2018I totally understand that dealers make mistakes sometimes. I\u2019m going to talk through everything I do. You\u2019ll see every step. If you have questions as I go, just ask.\u2019 This accomplishes three things: it validates their concern, it commits you to transparency, and it redirects their energy from challenging you to watching you work."));
body.push(para("When you handle this well, other players at the table will tell the skeptical player to back off and let you work. That peer pressure is far more effective than anything the dealer could say. But it only happens if you have earned the table\u2019s trust through clear communication, good chip handling, and professional composure. If you respond with arrogance, the skeptical player will feel justified, and the rest of the table will silently agree."));
body.push(h3("Requesting a Floor Supervisor"));
body.push(para("In any multi-way all-in involving four or more players, it is good practice to call a floor supervisor to the table proactively. Do not wait until you make a mistake \u2014 get the extra set of eyes there before you start building pots. Supervisors appreciate being called early. In the entire history of multi-way all-ins, a supervisor has never not come to the table for a nine-way all-in. They will be watching everything you do, which is a good thing \u2014 it protects you from errors and protects the players\u2019 money."));
body.push(h3("Players Showing Hands Prematurely"));
body.push(para("When multiple players are all-in but there are still active players with chips, the all-in players may start flipping their cards face-up out of excitement. The dealer must be preventative: before running out the remaining board cards, announce \u2018Do not show your hands yet, there is still action.\u2019 Say this before it happens, not after. Players in multi-way all-in situations are excited and will instinctively want to reveal their hands. If all-in players do expose their hands before remaining action is complete, the dealer must manage the situation but cannot force the cards back face-down \u2014 the information is already public."));

body.push(h2("6.11 Common Dealer Errors in Side Pot Scenarios"));
body.push(para("Putting chips into the wrong pot. When action continues after one player is all-in, subsequent bets must go into the side pot, not the main pot. Dealers who lose track of which pot is which will misallocate chips, resulting in incorrect payouts."));
body.push(para("Forgetting the rake. With the mental load of building multiple pots, the rake falls out of the workflow. Before pushing the final pot, always check the rake drop slot. Train this as a reflex. The worst outcome is pushing a massive multi-way pot and having a supervisor realize you took zero dollars for the house."));
body.push(para("Incorrect payouts when running it twice. If the table agrees to run the board twice (deal two separate sets of community cards), and one player is already all-in and eligible only for the main pot, that player is playing for one board on the main pot while the remaining players split the side pot across two boards. Dealers frequently miscalculate which player gets what from which pot on which board."));
body.push(para("Stacking pots during construction. Do not build neat stacks during pot construction in normal hands \u2014 scoop chips into a spread pot. Stacking takes longer, reduces the visual excitement of a big pot (which affects tips), and in Omaha Hi-Lo games, stacked pots can topple and intermingle with side pots. However, during multi-way all-ins with many pots, temporary stacking while building and verifying pot amounts is acceptable and often necessary to keep things organized."));
body.push(para("Freezing under pressure. A four-way all-in with different stack sizes requires creating three side pots under the gaze of players with tens of thousands of dollars at stake. Many dealers visibly slow down, make arithmetic errors, and lose the confidence of the table. This single scenario is the strongest argument for robotic dealing in terms of accuracy improvement."));
body.push(para("Gathering hands to the center. Collecting all-in players\u2019 hands to the center of the table or next to the board is a common bad habit from televised poker. It makes it nearly impossible to track which hand belongs to which player when pushing pots. Always leave hands in front of their respective players."));
body.push(para("Not having players push chips forward. When multiple players go all-in, the dealer must ask all players to push their stacks forward. If you do not verify that all chips are visible and forward, players can hide chips or pocket them. \u2018All right, everybody\u2019s all-in, I need you to push your chips forward please.\u2019"));
body.push(para("Miscounting when switching between pots. When going around the table taking amounts from multiple stacks, it is easy to lose track of which player you have already collected from. Clear your hands between each stack. If you get lost, count the chip stacks or markers \u2014 the number of red chips in a pot should match the number of players who contributed."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must calculate side pots instantly and correctly for any number of all-in players with any stack sizes. Must physically separate pot amounts on the table and maintain tracking of which players are eligible for which pots. Must handle running-it-twice scenarios with multi-pot eligibility. Must display pot totals and eligibility either physically or on a digital display. Must never forget the rake regardless of pot complexity. Must track all player stacks via sensors to prevent chip hiding. This is a pure computational task that a robotic system would handle with zero errors, eliminating the single most common source of dealer mistakes in live poker."));

body.push(h2("6.12 The Nine-Way All-In: Extreme Scenario"));
body.push(para("A nine-way all-in \u2014 every player at the table all-in with different stack sizes \u2014 is the most extreme scenario a dealer can face. It produces up to eight side pots. While rare in cash games (it requires every player to believe they have a legitimate chance of winning, which is hard to come by), it does happen, and a dealer must be prepared for it. In tournaments, multi-way all-ins are more common because short stacks create frequent all-in situations."));
body.push(para("The procedure is the same as any multi-way all-in, just with more repetitions. Start with the smallest stack. Take that amount from all nine players. Build the main pot. Find the next smallest stack. Take the difference from the remaining eight players. Build Side Pot 1. Continue through all eight side pots. Use chip markers for each pot, angled toward the eligible player."));
body.push(para("Practical realities of a nine-way all-in: It can take 15 to 45 minutes to resolve. The next dealer will be standing behind you waiting. It does not matter \u2014 take the time you need. A supervisor will absolutely be at the table; they always come for situations like this. Your chip-handling skills are on full display: cutting chips accurately, making change quickly, and keeping organized under pressure. This is why chip mechanics matter \u2014 they pay off in the moments when it matters most."));
body.push(para("Color-up during construction: If pot building is creating unmanageable numbers of small-denomination chips, you can color up within a pot. Take five red $5 chips out of a side pot and replace them with one green $25 chip from the bank. Be explicit about what you are doing: \u2018I\u2019m coloring up this pot to make it easier to manage.\u2019 This is perfectly acceptable and can prevent chip chaos in large multi-way pots."));
body.push(para("Tipping after large multi-way pots: Dealers should have zero expectation of a tip after resolving a complex multi-way all-in. Some players will tip generously. Some will forget entirely because they are overwhelmed by the result. Some will never tip regardless. If you build a tip expectation into your mental model, you create what poker players call a \u2018negative freeroll\u2019 \u2014 you can only have a negative emotional outcome, never a positive one. Focus on doing the job correctly. If a tip comes, be genuinely appreciative. If it does not, move on to the next hand."));

body.push(h2("6.13 Split Pots and Quartering"));
body.push(para("When two or more players tie for the best hand, the pot is split equally between them. Splitting is straightforward in a two-way tie: the dealer divides the pot into two equal stacks. Odd chips are awarded to the player \u2018closest to the button\u2019 \u2014 meaning the player who will receive the button soonest (worst position, first to act). This is the universal tiebreaker in casino poker. However, the complexity escalates dramatically in games with high-low splits and multiple boards."));
body.push(para("Critical rule for multi-pot split scenarios: the odd chip distribution is handled independently for each pot. If a main pot is split four ways and has three extra chips, each extra chip goes to the next-worst-position player. If a side pot between two players also has an odd chip, that chip goes to the worst-position player among the two eligible players \u2014 regardless of who received odd chips from other pots. A player can receive an extra chip from the main pot and another extra chip from a side pot in the same hand. The dealer must never combine pots and then distribute \u2014 each pot is resolved and split separately with its own odd chip assignment."));

body.push(h3("Quartering in PLO Double Board Bomb Pots"));
body.push(para("In Pot-Limit Omaha double board bomb pots (an increasingly common format in casino cash games), each board receives exactly half the pot. If one player wins the top board outright and two players tie on the bottom board, the top board winner receives 50% of the pot, and the two bottom board winners each receive 25%. This is called quartering. The player who gets quartered ends up with significantly less than they may expect, which is a frequent source of confusion and disputes."));
body.push(para("The dealing procedure for quartering: First, the dealer separates the total pot into two equal halves, one for each board. The winning player on Board 1 receives their half immediately. Then the dealer takes the remaining half and splits it again into two equal portions for the tied players on Board 2. This means the dealer is performing nested pot division: split the total pot, then split half of that split. With odd chips, the same left-of-button rule applies at each level of division."));
body.push(h3("River Bet Shortcut for Quartering"));
body.push(para("When there are bets on the final street before a quartering situation, the standard method of collecting everything into a central pot and then performing the nested division is slow and error-prone. Experienced dealers use a shortcut: they push the river bets directly to the winner of one board, then split the remaining pre-river pot between the other board\u2019s tied winners. This works because the river bets will mathematically end up in the same place regardless of whether you merge first or pay directly. The shortcut avoids combining and re-separating, which is the operation where chips most commonly end up in the wrong pile. Players get visibly nervous when a dealer has never handled a quartering situation before, and the confidence of the shortcut method matters for table trust."));
body.push(para("In PLO high-low split games (like Big O / PLO8), the complexity compounds further. Each board already splits between high and low winners. If two players tie for the low on one board while another player scoops the high, that board\u2019s half is first split high/low, then the low half is split again between the tying players. A single pot can be divided four, six, or even eight ways in extreme scenarios. Dealers report that these multi-way split calculations are the single most stressful dealing situation they encounter, and errors are common even among experienced dealers."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must handle nested pot divisions with arbitrary numbers of winners per board per direction (high/low). Must correctly apply odd-chip tiebreaker rules at each level of division. Must clearly display to all players exactly how much each person receives and from which pot portion. Must resolve nine-way all-ins with up to eight side pots in seconds rather than minutes. Must maintain a complete audit trail of every chip movement for surveillance review. This multi-dimensional split calculation is trivial for software but extraordinarily error-prone for human dealers."));

// ── 7. ERROR RECOVERY ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("7. Error Recovery and Misdeals"));
body.push(para("Error recovery is where the gap between experienced and inexperienced dealers is most visible. A seasoned dealer handles a misdeal smoothly in seconds. An inexperienced dealer freezes, calls the floor, and kills the table\u2019s energy for several minutes. A robotic system has an opportunity to handle most errors automatically and instantly, but must know the complete ruleset. The critical principle underlying all error recovery: all efforts go toward preserving the hand. Misdeals cost time, money, and disrupt game flow \u2014 they can affect high-hand promotion eligibility, blind level timing in tournaments, and player satisfaction. However, when a misdeal is genuinely required, the dealer must act decisively."));
body.push(para("A key distinction runs through all error recovery: most misdeal and error rulings are supervisor (floor) decisions, not dealer decisions. Dealers should not declare a hand dead on their own in ambiguous situations. The exceptions are clear-cut misdeal conditions described below. For everything else \u2014 call the floor, communicate what happened, and do not speculate on the outcome."));

body.push(h2("7.1 Misdeal Conditions"));
body.push(para("A misdeal is declared and the hand is completely restarted. Per TDA Rule 35, misdeal conditions include but are not limited to the situations below. In a misdeal redeal: the button does not move, players remain seated, limits stay the same, and the redeal counts as the same hand (not an additional hand for players on penalty in tournaments)."));

body.push(h3("Box Cards (Face-Up Cards in the Deck)"));
body.push(para("A box card is a card found face-up in the deck during the deal. One box card: the card goes directly into the muck (it is NOT treated as an exposed card and does not receive a replacement \u2014 it is simply removed from play). The dealer should immediately call a supervisor, because if a second box card appears later in the same hand, the hand is ruled dead and all money is reconstructed and returned to each player. Two or more box cards: automatic misdeal. The dealer says \u2018bring them back\u2019 and collects all cards. No supervisor call is needed for the declaration itself \u2014 two box cards is unambiguous. Common cause: a player\u2019s hand went face-up during collection and was shuffled in without correction. More common in games without shuffle machines."));

body.push(h3("Exposed Cards During the Initial Deal"));
body.push(para("If the first card off the deck flashes during the pitch, that is a misdeal. If the first card to either blind position is exposed, that is a misdeal. If two or more cards are exposed during the initial deal to any players, that is a misdeal. In five-card flop games (Hold\u2019em/Omaha), if one of the first two cards dealt off the deck is exposed, or if both of a player\u2019s down cards are exposed by dealer error, it is a misdeal. However, if only one card (other than the first card to the blinds) is exposed during the deal, it is NOT a misdeal \u2014 the exposed card is shown to all players, placed aside as the first burn card, and the affected player receives the next card off the deck as a replacement after the deal completes. Players may be dealt two consecutive cards on the button (this is acceptable, not a misdeal)."));

body.push(h3("Dealing to the Wrong Seat or Out of Sequence"));
body.push(para("If the first card is dealt to the wrong player (e.g., the button instead of the player left of the big blind), that is a misdeal. If a player is skipped entirely during the deal, that is a misdeal (provided the skipped player speaks up before substantial action). Quick recovery exception: if the dealer immediately notices a card went to the wrong seat and is 100% certain no player has looked at any card and the cards can be slid back to their correct positions, the dealer may correct the error silently. However, if there is even a tiny chance a card was seen or the order was disrupted, it must be a misdeal. Players will report any integrity shortcut to supervisors, and rightfully so."));

body.push(h3("Dealing Extra Seats or Missing Players \u2014 Cash vs. Tournament"));
body.push(para("Dealing an extra hand to an empty seat or ineligible player: In a cash game, this is NOT a misdeal. The dealer kills (mucks) those cards and the hand continues. The reasoning is rooted in random card theory \u2014 all cards are random after a proper shuffle, so dealing two extra cards to a dead seat does not mathematically affect any other player\u2019s hand. In a tournament, dealing in an ineligible player IS a misdeal. Cash game procedure note: if the dealer realizes on the first round of cards that an extra seat received a card, the dealer does NOT skip that seat on the second round. Instead, the dealer deals the second card to the extra seat as well (completing a full hand), then kills that entire hand. This preserves player expectations about card order. Dealing in an absent player: in a cash game, if the absent player returns before their turn to act, the hand is live; otherwise their hand is killed. It is not a misdeal. In a tournament, dealing in an absent player who should not receive cards is a misdeal."));

body.push(h3("Incorrect Card Count"));
body.push(para("If a player receives too few cards and the error is caught before action: the dealer gives the player the top card off the deck. If the shorted card got intermingled with another player\u2019s cards and cannot be reliably identified, that is a misdeal. If a player receives too many cards before any action: misdeal. If a player discovers they have too many cards after action has begun: their hand is dead (not a misdeal \u2014 the rest of the hand plays out). Posting a blind or straddle does NOT count as action for this determination."));

body.push(h3("Deck Integrity Issues"));
body.push(para("Discovery of a joker, missing cards, duplicate cards (e.g., two queens of diamonds from a defective deck), or wrong-colored backs: supervisor call. Fundamental deck integrity issues void the hand and require full money reconstruction regardless of how far into the hand the discovery occurs \u2014 even after substantial action. This is the one category that overrides the substantial action threshold. A defective card (bent corner, distinguishable back) is a supervisor judgment call \u2014 a minor bend may not kill the hand, but a clearly identifiable mark will."));

body.push(h3("Other Misdeal Conditions"));
body.push(para("Failure to properly shuffle or cut the deck before dealing is a misdeal if caught before substantial action. Dealing extra community cards in tempo (e.g., a dealer switching from Omaha to Hold\u2019em accidentally deals 3+ hole cards) is a misdeal if the extra cards went beyond just the burn card. A game played with antes where the pot is found short one or more antes is NOT a misdeal \u2014 the offender posts the missing ante and the hand plays out."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must maintain a complete state machine of all misdeal conditions, distinguishing between cash game and tournament rules. Must detect box cards, exposed cards, incorrect card counts, and dealing sequence errors in real time. Must implement the extra-seat procedure correctly for cash games (deal the full hand, then kill it). Must log every misdeal with full context for regulatory review."));

body.push(h2("7.2 Substantial Action \u2014 The Point of No Return"));
body.push(para("Substantial action is the threshold after which a misdeal can no longer be declared and the hand must play out with corrections applied. TDA defines substantial action as either: (1) three players taking any action (including folds), or (2) two players acting by bet, call, or raise. Significant actions include: a fold, a bet, a call, a raise, a verbal declaration, a dealer burning a card, and a dealer dealing community cards. Posting a blind or straddle does NOT count as action for substantial action purposes. Once substantial action has occurred, the hand is locked in: skipped players lose their right to a hand, players with too many cards have their hand killed (but the rest continues), and misdeal conditions result in corrections rather than restarts. The one exception: fundamental deck integrity issues override substantial action and require full hand reconstruction."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must track an action counter in real time, classifying each action type. Must know exactly when substantial action has been reached and adjust all error recovery procedures accordingly. This is a straightforward state-tracking problem that eliminates the ambiguity human dealers face when reconstructing whether \u2018enough\u2019 action occurred."));

body.push(h2("7.3 Action Out of Turn"));
body.push(para("Action out of turn occurs when a player acts before it is their turn. This is one of the most disruptive errors in poker because it leaks information and can give unfair advantages. The dealer is the last line of defense, and aggressive, immediate intervention is required."));
body.push(h3("Dealer Response Protocol"));
body.push(para("The dealer must follow the action at all times \u2014 eyes on the \u2018window\u2019 where the active player\u2019s chips, cards, and hands are. When the dealer sees a player act out of turn, the response must be immediate and assertive: physically hold up a hand if necessary, verbally say \u2018Hold on \u2014 action is over here,\u2019 and stop the chain before additional players act. Each additional out-of-turn action compounds the problem. Once stopped, the dealer directs the correct player: \u2018It\u2019s your turn. Act as if this wasn\u2019t here.\u2019 The dealer does not explain consequences \u2014 if the player insists on knowing the rules, the dealer calls the floor rather than getting drawn into a discussion."));
body.push(h3("Binding and Non-Binding Rules (TDA Rule 53)"));
body.push(para("An out-of-turn action (check, call, or raise) is backed up to the correct player. The out-of-turn action is BINDING if the action to the out-of-turn player does not change \u2014 meaning the amount they would have to call is the same as when they acted. A check, call, or fold by the correct player does NOT change the action, so the out-of-turn bet stands. If the correct player bets or raises (changing the amount), the out-of-turn action is NOT binding \u2014 chips are returned and the player has all options. An out-of-turn fold is always binding \u2014 the player cannot reclaim their cards. In tournaments, acting out of turn is also subject to penalty."));
body.push(h3("Multiple Players Acting Out of Turn"));
body.push(para("If multiple players act out of turn in sequence, the dealer calls the floor immediately. The supervisor\u2019s ruling is based on when the dealer knew and attempted to stop the action. If the dealer was actively calling out \u2018hold on\u2019 and players kept going, the supervisor may back up every action. For skipped players who allowed substantial action before speaking up: the supervisor may rule their hand dead or limit them to non-aggressive action only (call or fold, no raise)."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must enforce strict turn order \u2014 only accept input from the active player. Player-specific input zones that activate only during that player\u2019s turn would eliminate this entire error category. Must track whether the action has changed for binding/non-binding determination. Must log all out-of-turn attempts for pattern detection (repeated offenders may be angle-shooting)."));

body.push(h2("7.4 Premature Cards (Premature Board Cards)"));
body.push(para("A premature card is a community card dealt before action on the previous betting round is complete. This is one of the most consequential dealer errors. Every premature card ruling must be handled by a supervisor \u2014 no exceptions. The dealer\u2019s only job is to call the floor and communicate what happened. Critical communication rule: the dealer must NEVER tell players what the ruling will be. The correct statement is: \u2018I\u2019m calling the floor. They\u2019re going to make a decision.\u2019 Setting player expectations that the supervisor may not fulfill creates a nightmare for the supervisor."));
body.push(h3("Premature Turn Procedure (Most Common Scenario)"));
body.push(para("Example: flop dealt, player bets 300, two players call, but the dealer misses a fourth player with cards. Dealer burns and deals the turn. Fourth player speaks up. Procedure: (1) Dealer calls floor immediately. (2) Premature turn card is set aside face-up. (3) Remaining flop action must complete first \u2014 the missed player acts on the flop. (4) Once flop action is done, dealer burns a new card and deals the \u2018natural river\u2019 as the new turn. (5) After turn action, the premature card goes back into the stub. (6) The stub (including the returned card) is washed, shuffled, and cut WITHOUT exposing any cards \u2014 the dealer must pick up stub cards carefully, not square them by lifting and tapping (which risks exposing faces). (7) A river is dealt from the shuffled stub WITHOUT an additional burn card. The supervisor stays through the entire process."));
body.push(h3("Premature Flop and River Procedures"));
body.push(para("Premature flop (dealt before pre-flop action completes): flop burn card is left as the burn, the three premature flop cards are returned to the stub, the entire stub is reshuffled, and after pre-flop action completes the flop is dealt from the new stub without another burn. Premature river (dealt before turn action completes): the premature river card goes back into the stub, the river\u2019s burn card is left as the burn, the stub is reshuffled after turn action, and a new river is dealt without another burn."));
body.push(h3("The Half-Probability Problem (Industry Critique)"));
body.push(para("The standard procedure has a known mathematical flaw. When a premature turn card is set aside and the \u2018natural river\u2019 becomes the turn, the premature card is only shuffled back in for the river draw. It has ONE chance to appear on the board, while every other stub card has TWO chances (turn and river). The premature card has half the probability of making it onto the board. The better solution (advocated by dealing instructors like Mark Shumaker, not yet adopted by TDA): reshuffle the entire stub immediately when it is time to deal the turn, giving the premature card equal probability. Nobody saw the natural river, so preserving it has zero integrity value. TDA\u2019s Recommended Procedure 14 (2019) on random card theory suggests the industry is moving this direction."));
body.push(h3("Premature Card Prevention"));
body.push(para("Root cause is almost always the dealer not following the action. Common scenarios: (1) player is thinking quietly and dealer assumes they checked \u2014 silence is not a check; (2) player\u2019s cards are hidden behind chips and dealer doesn\u2019t realize they\u2019re still in; (3) dealer loses track during a long action sequence. The dealer should count active players at the start of each street."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must make premature cards structurally impossible by refusing to deal the next board card until all active players have provided input for the current betting round. This eliminates an entire category of consequential errors by design. If a system fault causes a premature deal, must implement the full TDA recovery procedure including stub reshuffling. Should implement the mathematically correct reshuffle method (full stub reshuffle) rather than the flawed standard procedure, pending regulatory approval."));

body.push(h2("7.5 Exposed Card Procedures (After Action Has Started)"));
body.push(para("Once meaningful action has occurred, the hand cannot be declared a misdeal. If a player\u2019s hole card is accidentally exposed by the dealer after the deal, the card is shown to the entire table and the player keeps it. If a community card is accidentally exposed during a board deal, the exposed card is shown to all and set aside, the dealer continues with the next card off the deck, and the exposed card becomes the next burn card or is shuffled back into the stub depending on house rules."));

body.push(h2("7.6 The Stub Count"));
body.push(para("The stub count is a game protection measure that verifies all 52 cards are accounted for. Dealers count the stub at least once per down (a 30-minute dealing session). Some state regulations (e.g., Florida, North Carolina) require stub counting even with shuffle machines; frequency varies from every hand to one-to-three times per down."));
body.push(para("The math: In a 10-player Hold\u2019em hand to the river, 28 cards are used (20 hole + 3 burns + 5 community), leaving 24 in the stub. The dealer counts in groups of 8: three groups of 8 equals 24. For 9 players: 26 remain (three 8s plus 2). For 8 players: 28 remain (three 8s plus 4). Formula: stub = 52 \u2212 (players \u00d7 2) \u2212 8. For incomplete hands (ended before river), add 2 per undealt street (one burn + one community card). Counting is done silently during river action."));
body.push(para("If the count is wrong: possible causes are a card stuck to another card (most common), card on the floor, card palmed by a player or dealer (most serious), or card left in a player\u2019s possession. The floor must be called immediately. The table may need to be searched, and in serious cases the hand may be voided."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must maintain a real-time count of all cards dealt, burned, and remaining. Must verify 52-card integrity every hand. Must track the exact identity of every card (not just count), enabling immediate identification of which specific card is missing. Must alert operators immediately on any discrepancy. This is trivial for a computer but a non-trivial game protection task for a human dealer."));

// ── 8. CHIP HANDLING ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("8. Chip Handling and Bank Management"));
body.push(para("Chip handling is a core physical skill that every dealer must master. It involves cutting chips (separating a stack into specific quantities), making change, processing buy-ins, managing the chip bank (the dealer\u2019s chip tray), and executing color-ups (exchanging small denomination chips for larger ones). The standards exist for speed, accuracy, and surveillance verification."));

body.push(h2("8.1 Cutting Chips"));
body.push(para("Chips are broken down (cut) in standardized groupings for verification. A full stack in any casino is 20 chips of a single denomination. The breakdown groupings are industry standard and exist to make visual verification instantaneous for the dealer, surveillance, and players:"));
body.push(para("Cut in groups of 5: $1 chips ($5 per group, $100 per full stack), $2 chips ($10 per group, $40 per full stack), $5 chips ($25 per group, $100 per full stack), $100 chips ($500 per group, $2,000 per full stack). Cut in groups of 4: $0.50 chips ($2 per group, $10 per full stack), $25 chips ($100 per group, $500 per full stack), $500 chips ($2,000 per group, $10,000 per full stack). These groupings are chosen so that each cut-group equals a clean dollar amount, making pot calculations and change-making faster."));
body.push(h3("The Bump-and-Prove Technique"));
body.push(para("The physical chip cutting technique, as detailed in TruePokerDealer Lesson 5a, follows a specific sequence called \u2018bump and prove\u2019:"));
body.push(para("Step 1 (Grip): The dealer uses their index finger (considered the most professional method; some dealers use their thumb, but the index finger provides a higher speed ceiling and greater range of motion). The grip has the thumb on one side of the stack, middle finger on the other side, and pinky behind the stack protecting it from shifting backward. The index finger is lifted and positioned flat on top."));
body.push(para("Step 2 (Initial cut): The dealer picks up all chips except the bottom 5 (or 4, depending on denomination). If they accidentally take one too many or one too few, they simply adjust. With practice, dealers get the correct count on the first try most of the time."));
body.push(para("Step 3 (Placement): The lifted stack is placed slightly behind the remaining stack (during training; at speed, it is placed close). This is where the pinky protecting the back becomes critical: without it, the stack will shift backward when pushed forward."));
body.push(para("Step 4 (Bump): The dealer pushes the held stack forward into the stationary stack. This is the \u2018bump.\u2019 The physical contact between the two stacks stabilizes them and creates a reference point for the next step."));
body.push(para("Step 5 (Prove): The dealer slides the index finger flat across the top of the held stack toward the stationary stack. When the index finger makes contact with the stationary stack at the same height as the held stack, this \u2018proves\u2019 that both stacks are the same height (and therefore the same count). The dealer then lifts the proved portion and drops the next group of 5 (or 4)."));
body.push(para("Step 6 (Repeat): Bump and prove all the way down. After the final drop, the dealer proves one more time even though there are no more chips to pull up. This final prove verifies that the last dropped group matches all the others. Without this final prove, the last group could be off by one chip and the error would go undetected."));
body.push(para("Step 7 (Splash): The dealer takes the proven stacks between thumb and middle finger at a slight angle and pushes them off the felt in a fanning motion. This \u2018splash\u2019 separates the groups horizontally so that each group of 5 (or 4) is visually distinct and countable at a glance. Do not spin or drop the chips; if they are sticky (common with heavily used casino chips), spinning causes them to fly apart."));
body.push(para("Step 8 (Clear and verify): The dealer clears their hands (shows palms). Then they scoop the chips back up with thumb on one side, middle finger on the other, ring finger and pinky behind, and prove one final time as they pick up. This final prove ensures no chip was palmed during the splash. Then the chips are handed off or placed in the tray."));
body.push(h3("The Moment-of-Contact Principle"));
body.push(para("An advanced detail frequently missed in basic training: the accuracy of a chip cut depends on executing the separation at the exact moment the moving stack makes contact with the stationary stack. When the dealer pushes a portion of chips forward and they bump into the previous stack, that contact stabilizes the chip heights and ensures the correct count separates cleanly. Training yourself to cut at that precise instant of contact, rather than cutting mid-slide, dramatically improves accuracy, especially with worn or sticky casino chips that resist separation. At speed, the bump is nearly invisible, but it is always present in a professional cut."));
body.push(h3("Chip Geometry Note"));
body.push(para("A surprising physical property of casino chips relevant to visual verification systems: a single chip stood on its edge is exactly 12 chips tall when stacked. This means a standard chip is almost exactly a perfect square when viewed from the side (the diameter-to-height ratio of stacked chips is 12:1, and a single chip on edge equals 12 stacked). This property is consistent across virtually all casino chip manufacturers and is useful for calibrating visual chip-counting systems."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must separate chip stacks into standard casino groupings (5s or 4s depending on denomination). Must identify denominations by color, markings, or RFID. Must execute cuts quickly and display results clearly for verification. Must maintain organized chip bank with denominations in standard order. Must learn both hands (the bump-and-prove technique must be executable from either side of the tray). All chip movements must be camera-friendly for surveillance verification."));

body.push(h2("8.2 Chip Handling Security Protocols"));
body.push(para("Every chip interaction is a potential theft vector, and dealers are trained in specific handling protocols designed to make theft physically impossible and immediately detectable by surveillance."));
body.push(h3("Clearing Hands"));
body.push(para("Throughout any chip transaction, the dealer must periodically clear their hands by briefly showing both palms to the ceiling and the backs of their hands. This proves to surveillance cameras that no chips are being palmed. The core rule: any time the dealer interacts with chips or money and then moves their hands away from the table or toward their body, they must clear. Specific triggers include: after cutting chips, after completing a buy-in, after pushing a pot, after dropping the rake, after putting a tip in the box, any time hands come out of the chip tray, and critically, any time the dealer touches their face, body, or any personal surface. The sequence is: push pot, clear hands, then reach toward your body. Enter the well, come out, clear hands before touching anything else."));
body.push(para("Surveillance enforcement of this protocol is strict: one experienced dealer in the TruePokerDealer training series reports receiving over 40 write-ups across his career, with a significant portion being for clearing hands violations. Even veteran dealers get written up for unconsciously skipping the clear during high-speed action. In the state of Florida, as of 2016, state gaming inspectors would conduct unannounced evaluations specifically checking for hand clearing compliance. Dealers who were caught not clearing could receive a fine of $50 to $100 mailed directly to them by the state \u2014 not from the casino, from the government. This illustrates how seriously some jurisdictions take this protocol. Getting the hand clearing habit ingrained early is essential because breaking bad habits later is extremely difficult, and transferring from a room that does not enforce clearing to one that does will expose every gap in the habit immediately."));
body.push(h3("Fingertip Rule"));
body.push(para("Chips must always be held in the fingertips, never in the palm. When holding a single chip, the thumb is on one side and the index, middle, and ring fingers on the other, with visible space in the palm. When holding a stack, the same principle applies: the chips are controlled by the fingers with the palm open. This prevents a dealer from concealing a chip in their hand during any transaction."));
body.push(h3("No Hand-to-Hand Transfers"));
body.push(para("A dealer must never transfer chips directly from one hand to the other. If a chip or stack needs to move from the right hand to the left hand, the dealer places it on the felt, clears the first hand, then picks it up with the second hand. This eliminates the possibility of a chip being palmed during the transfer. While slightly slower, the drop-clear-pickup motion becomes nearly as fast as a direct transfer with practice. New dealers frequently violate this rule unconsciously, and surveillance will issue write-ups."));
body.push(h3("The Roll Technique for Tray Placement"));
body.push(para("When placing a stack of chips into the dealer tray, the natural grip (fingers on the sides) makes it physically difficult to insert the stack into the narrow tray slot. Dealers learn a specific roll technique: place the thumb on top of the stack, fingers on the bottom, and rotate the stack toward themselves so it can be lowered vertically into the tray slot. If the grip is too loose during the roll, the chips scatter across the tray or felt. This is a frequently fumbled operation for new dealers and a source of embarrassment during auditions. The roll must become muscle memory before it can be performed at game speed."));
body.push(h3("Never Take Anything from a Player\u2019s Hand"));
body.push(para("In casino operations, dealers never take any item directly from a player\u2019s hand. If a player wants to hand over a player\u2019s card, cash, or any other item, the dealer instructs them to place it on the felt first: \u2018Put that down on the table, please.\u2019 The player sets it down, and then the dealer picks it up. This applies to money, identification, tickets, and any other item. The rule exists for surveillance purposes: hand-to-hand transfers between dealers and players are nearly impossible to track on camera, creating opportunities for both theft and false accusations."));
body.push(h3("No Unnecessary Chip Handling"));
body.push(para("Dealers must not handle customer or house chips unnecessarily. A common bad habit: dealers will take a few chips from the pot during action and idly shuffle them while waiting for players to act. This is an absolute prohibition in security-conscious rooms. Any unnecessary chip manipulation increases the opportunity for theft and creates surveillance tracking problems. Similarly, dealers should not fidget with gaming equipment (cards, cut cards, buttons) when not performing a dealing function. Some rooms enforce this strictly with write-ups; others are more lenient. The danger of developing the habit in a lenient room is that it becomes extremely hard to break when transferring to a strict room."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "A robotic system has an inherent advantage in chip security because its manipulators cannot palm chips. However, the system must still provide visible audit trail of all chip movements. Every chip picked up must be traceable to where it is placed. The system should log all transactions for surveillance review and provide camera-friendly chip handling that surveillance staff can verify at a glance. A robotic system would never engage in unnecessary chip handling, eliminating this category of surveillance concern entirely."));

body.push(h2("8.3 The Bank"));
body.push(para("The dealer\u2019s chip bank (tray) is a structured rack that holds the house chips. Standard arrangement places lower denominations on the left (from the dealer\u2019s perspective) and higher denominations on the right. Bank maintenance is critical: a messy bank slows every transaction and increases errors. When the bank runs low on a denomination, the dealer requests a fill from the chip runner. When the bank has excess chips, a credit is removed."));

// ── 9. GAME VARIATIONS ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("9. Game Variations and Mixed Games"));
body.push(para("Texas Hold\u2019em is the most common poker variant in casino environments and the baseline for any robotic dealer system. However, a complete deployment specification must account for the other games commonly dealt in casino poker rooms, because a system limited to Hold\u2019em would not be sufficient for a typical casino poker room operation."));

body.push(h2("9.1 Texas Hold\u2019em (No-Limit and Limit)"));
body.push(para("2 hole cards per player, 5 community cards (3-1-1), 4 betting rounds. No-limit allows any bet up to a player\u2019s full stack. Limit restricts bets to fixed amounts (small bet on first two rounds, big bet on last two). The dealing procedure is identical; only the betting rules differ."));

body.push(h2("9.2 Pot-Limit Omaha (PLO)"));
body.push(para("4 hole cards per player (doubles the dealing time per hand), 5 community cards, 4 betting rounds. Maximum bet is the size of the pot. The dealer must be able to calculate the pot size at any time, which is non-trivial when there have been multiple raises. Players must use exactly 2 of their 4 hole cards and exactly 3 community cards to make their best hand. This hand-reading requirement is significantly more complex than Hold\u2019em and is where many dealer errors occur at showdown."));
body.push(h3("Omaha Hand-Reading Complexity"));
body.push(para("The \u201Cexactly 2 from hand, exactly 3 from board\u201D rule is the single most common source of showdown errors in Omaha games. Players and inexperienced dealers frequently misread hands by using 1 or 3 cards from the hand. Professional dealer training teaches a systematic evaluation approach: first, check if the board is paired (if yes, full houses are possible). Second, check for flush possibilities. Third, check for straights by identifying which specific card combinations would complete them. Then evaluate sets and two-pair. For each player, the dealer mentally tests all possible 2-card combinations from the hole cards against the board."));
body.push(h3("The Framing Technique"));
body.push(para("Experienced Omaha dealers use a physical technique called framing to verify hands at showdown. When a potential winning hand is identified, the dealer physically pulls the relevant 2 hole cards and 3 board cards together, separating them from the remaining cards, to visually confirm the hand. This prevents the most common error: declaring a winner based on a hand that uses 3 cards from the player\u2019s hand or 4 from the board. Framing adds a few seconds per showdown but eliminates the most costly category of dealer errors. In practice sessions, dealers are trained to frame every hand until it becomes instinctive, then gradually speed up while maintaining accuracy."));
body.push(para("The math: with 4 hole cards, there are 6 possible 2-card combinations. With 5 community cards, there are 10 possible 3-card combinations. That yields 60 possible 5-card hands per player. At a full table of 10 players, the dealer must potentially evaluate up to 600 hand combinations at showdown. In practice, the dealer reads the board first to identify what hands are possible and then looks for the relevant cards in each player\u2019s hand, but the cognitive load is still enormous compared to Hold\u2019em."));
body.push(h3("Pot-Limit Calculation"));
body.push(para("In pot-limit games, the maximum bet is the current pot size plus the amount needed to call. This means the dealer must track the pot total continuously. After multiple raises in a single round, the pot-limit calculation becomes: pot before action + all previous bets in this round + the amount needed to call the current bet. Dealers are frequently asked \u201Cwhat\u2019s the pot?\u201D and must answer immediately. In round-up raking environments, odd amounts from previous pot calculations make tracking even harder. This is one of the most requested features for dealer assistance technology."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must deal 4 cards per player (up to 40 cards for a full table). Must calculate pot-limit maximum bets in real time and announce them on request. Must correctly evaluate 4-card hands using exactly 2 hole cards and 3 community cards at showdown, which requires evaluating 60 possible 5-card combinations per player. Must never misapply the Omaha hand-reading rule. This is perhaps the strongest single argument for robotic dealing accuracy over human dealing."));

body.push(h2("9.3 Omaha Hi-Lo and Big O (PLO8 / 5-Card PLO8)"));
body.push(para("In Omaha Hi-Lo (8 or better), the pot is split between the best high hand and the best qualifying low hand (five unpaired cards 8 or below, using exactly 2 from hand and 3 from board for each direction independently). Big O is the 5-card variant, giving each player 5 hole cards instead of 4, further increasing the combinatorial complexity. These games are spreading rapidly in casino poker rooms and are among the most dealer-error-prone formats in existence."));
body.push(para("The dealer must evaluate each player\u2019s hand twice: once for the high and once for the low. A player can use different 2-card combinations from their hand for each direction. If no player has a qualifying low (five unpaired cards 8 or below), the high hand scoops the entire pot. The dealer must determine whether a qualifying low exists before splitting, which requires checking every player\u2019s possible low combinations against the board. With 5-card Big O, there are 10 possible 2-card combinations per player, yielding 100 possible 5-card hands per player per direction. At a full table, this is 2,000 hand evaluations at showdown."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must evaluate high and low hands independently for every player. Must correctly determine low qualification (8 or better, no pairs). Must handle scoops (no qualifying low) and split pots. Must support 4-card and 5-card Omaha variants. Hand evaluation complexity makes this the game variant where robotic accuracy provides the most dramatic improvement over human dealing."));

body.push(h2("9.4 Seven-Card Stud"));
body.push(para("No community cards. Each player receives 7 individual cards over 5 rounds of dealing (2 face-down, 1 face-up initially, then 3 more face-up one at a time, then 1 final face-down). Action starts with the lowest visible card (the bring-in) and on subsequent rounds with the highest visible hand. The dealer must read visible hands to determine betting order, which changes every round. There are no blinds; instead, all players post an ante and there is a mandatory bring-in."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must deal cards face-up and face-down in alternating sequences across multiple rounds. Must read visible hands to determine betting order. Must track antes and bring-in bets. Must manage up to 56 cards in play simultaneously (8 players x 7 cards). This game pushes the limits of table space and card tracking."));

body.push(h2("9.5 Mixed Games (H.O.R.S.E. and Dealer\u2019s Choice)"));
body.push(para("Mixed game formats rotate between different poker variants on a fixed schedule (typically every orbit of the dealer button or every 30 minutes). H.O.R.S.E. rotates through Hold\u2019em, Omaha Hi-Lo, Razz, Seven-Card Stud, and Stud Eight-or-Better. The dealer must switch between completely different dealing procedures, hand rankings, betting structures, and showdown rules every 8 to 10 hands. This is the most demanding format for human dealers and the format most likely to produce errors."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must store and switch between complete rule sets for all standard poker variants. Must change dealing procedure, hand evaluation, betting structure, and showdown rules on command. Must clearly announce which game is being played to all players at each rotation. This is a significant software architecture requirement: the system must be modular enough to hot-swap game logic without interrupting operation."));

body.push(h2("9.6 Double Board Bomb Pots"));
body.push(para("An increasingly common format in casino cash games, particularly at higher stakes. In a bomb pot, all players post a predetermined ante (typically 2x to 5x the big blind), there is no preflop betting, and the flop is dealt immediately. In double board bomb pots, two separate sets of community cards are dealt side by side. Each board receives exactly half the pot. This format creates some of the largest and most complex pots a dealer will encounter."));
body.push(para("The dealer manages two parallel sets of community cards, dealing each street to both boards before proceeding. At showdown, the dealer must evaluate each player\u2019s best hand against each board independently. Players can win one board, both boards (a scoop), or tie on either. When ties occur on one board while another player wins the other outright, quartering situations arise. The combination of large pots, multiple boards, and potential quartering makes this the highest-complexity dealing scenario in modern cash games."));
body.push(para("Double board bomb pots can use any poker variant as the base game. Hold\u2019em double board bomb pots are common, but PLO and Big O double board bomb pots also occur. When combined with hi-lo split games, a single pot can theoretically be divided eight ways: high and low on each of two boards, with ties possible in each direction. These extreme scenarios are rare but must be handled correctly when they occur."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must manage two parallel community card layouts simultaneously. Must correctly divide the pot between boards and handle nested splits (quartering, hi-lo on each board). Must track player eligibility per board (all players are eligible for both boards in standard format). Must display clearly which board is being evaluated during showdown. The computational complexity is trivial for software but represents the most stressful scenario for human dealers."));

// ── 10. PLAYER MANAGEMENT ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("10. Player Interaction and Table Management"));
body.push(para("A poker dealer is not just a card-and-chip machine. They are the social center of the table, responsible for managing player behavior, enforcing rules, and maintaining the pace and energy of the game. This is the area where robotic dealers face the most significant design challenges, because it involves interpreting ambiguous human behavior in real time."));

body.push(h2("10.1 String Bets"));
body.push(para("A string bet occurs when a player puts out chips in multiple motions without verbally declaring their full action. For example, a player reaches for chips, places $50 on the table, reaches back to their stack, and places another $50. Without a verbal declaration of \u201Craise to $100,\u201D the second motion may be ruled a string bet and the player\u2019s action is limited to the first motion only ($50 call or bet). The dealer must detect this in real time and enforce the rule before the next player acts. This is one of the most common disputes in live poker."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must track player chip movements and detect multi-motion bets. Must correlate verbal declarations with physical actions. Must enforce the single-motion rule or verbal declaration rule per house rules. This requires both vision-based motion tracking and audio processing working in concert."));

body.push(h2("10.2 Out-of-Turn Action"));
body.push(para("If a player acts before it is their turn (bets, folds, or declares an action), the dealer must recognize this immediately and enforce the appropriate rule. An out-of-turn fold is always binding (the cards are dead). An out-of-turn bet or raise is binding if the action does not change when it returns to the correct player \u2014 meaning the amount to call is the same. If the correct player bets or raises (changing the amount), the out-of-turn action is not binding and the player gets all options back. See Section 7.3 for the complete dealer protocol, TDA Rule 53 details, and the procedure for handling chain reactions when multiple players act out of turn. The table management dimension: out-of-turn action is often the first sign that the dealer has lost control of the game. The assertive, immediate response (\u2018Hold on \u2014 action is over here\u2019) is not optional; it is the dealer\u2019s primary obligation. A dealer who quietly lets the action play out is failing the table."));

body.push(h2("10.3 Verbal Declarations vs Physical Actions"));
body.push(para("In casino poker, verbal declarations are binding. If a player says \u201Craise\u201D they must raise, even if they have not yet put chips forward. If a player says \u201Call\u201D and then tries to put out a raise, they are held to the call. The dealer must hear and register verbal declarations and enforce them even if the player\u2019s subsequent physical action contradicts them."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must have speech recognition capable of detecting poker-specific verbal declarations (check, bet, call, raise, fold, all-in) in noisy casino environments with multiple simultaneous conversations, music, and ambient noise. Must timestamp verbal declarations and correlate them with subsequent physical actions. False positive rate must be extremely low: incorrectly attributing a verbal declaration could cost a player thousands of dollars."));

body.push(h2("10.4 Reading the Customer"));
body.push(para("Dealers are expected to \u2018read the customer\u2019 \u2014 observing subtle clues about each player\u2019s mood, patience level, personality, and wants, then adjusting their interaction style accordingly. If a player clearly wants silence, the dealer deals in silence. If a player is superstitious (common in poker), the dealer respects those superstitions (e.g., not touching the player\u2019s chips if they\u2019re superstitious about it). The goal is that every player leaves the casino with a positive impression, regardless of their results."));
body.push(para("An important unwritten rule in poker dealing: the dealer should never look at a player\u2019s face while that player is in action (making a betting decision). This is a game integrity issue \u2014 the dealer\u2019s gaze could give away information or make the player uncomfortable. When it is not a player\u2019s turn and they are interacting with the dealer about something unrelated to the hand, normal eye contact is appropriate. But during action, the dealer\u2019s eyes stay on the chips, the cards, or the table."));
body.push(para("The most difficult reading situation occurs when players at the table contradict each other. Poker players are competitive and sometimes deliberately try to create conflict or tilt other players. The dealer must navigate these situations without taking sides, appearing to favor anyone, or escalating the tension. When contradictions become confrontational, the floor is called."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "A robotic system would not need to \u2018read\u2019 customers in the emotional sense, but it must be able to detect and respond to player states: waiting for action, engaged in conversation (do not interrupt), visibly agitated (notify floor staff), absent from seat (skip in dealing), and attempting to communicate with the dealer (respond to questions about pot size, action, etc.). The system should never direct cameras or sensors at a player\u2019s face during their action, respecting the same privacy principle that human dealers follow."));
body.push(h2("10.5 Player Pace and Game Energy"));
body.push(para("One of the most underappreciated aspects of dealing is how the dealer\u2019s energy and pace affect the entire table. A slow, disengaged dealer kills the game. Players leave, new players refuse to sit, and the room loses rake revenue. A fast, professional dealer keeps the game lively and players engaged. Professional dealers average approximately 30 hands per hour. Elite dealers can reach 35 or more. The difference between 25 hands per hour and 35 hands per hour across a full day represents a significant revenue impact for the casino and a significant experience difference for players."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must maintain a consistent pace of 30 to 40 hands per hour. Must not have variable speed (slowing down when situations get complex). Speed must be adjustable based on the game type and table composition. Must never create dead time between hands: the shuffle, deal, and pot push should be seamless."));

body.push(h2("10.6 Running the Game: The Four Dealer Instincts"));
body.push(para("Running the game is the most important skill a dealer develops, yet it is almost never taught in dealer school. It encompasses everything beyond the mechanical dealing \u2014 managing personalities, defusing confrontation, maintaining integrity under pressure, and projecting confidence. Experienced dealing instructors identify four fundamental instincts that guide every decision at the table:"));
body.push(para("Instinct 1 \u2014 Follow the Action with 100% Certainty: Not 99.9% \u2014 100%. The dealer must know with absolute certainty what every player said and did. When uncertain about a verbal declaration in a noisy environment, the dealer MUST get clarification immediately, even though asking a player to repeat themselves feels uncomfortable (it gives away information by forcing the player to speak again). The alternative \u2014 assuming what was said \u2014 puts the entire table at risk. Following the action is also the dealer\u2019s primary defensive weapon: when a player challenges the dealer, the dealer who saw everything can articulate exactly what happened, which shuts down angle-shooting and earns the respect of every other player at the table."));
body.push(para("Instinct 2 \u2014 Tie Every Decision to the Fundamental Rules: When deciding whether to speak up or let something go, the dealer filters through two questions: Does this violate the one-player-per-hand rule? Does this violate the cards-speak rule? If a player is talking about the board (\u2018three to a straight, I wonder if anyone has a four\u2019), that potentially violates one-player-per-hand because it conveys information or solicits reactions from active players. The dealer says: \u2018That violates one player per hand.\u2019 This framing is extremely hard for the player to argue with. If a player asks the dealer how much a clearly visible bet is, instead of answering verbally (which might create a confrontation), the dealer can simply spread the chips out so they are clearly visible \u2014 using the principle that all players deserve equal access to table information."));
body.push(para("Instinct 3 \u2014 Maintain Integrity Without Exception: Own mistakes immediately and openly. When a dealer makes an error and says \u2018I messed up, guys\u2019 matter-of-factly, the table\u2019s response is almost always respect. Trying to hide or minimize a mistake destroys trust and invites future challenges. Integrity also means not allowing confrontation, shot-taking, or rule violations to slide because it\u2019s easier to ignore them. A dealer who lets things go trains the table to push boundaries."));
body.push(para("Instinct 4 \u2014 Make the Table Like and Respect You: This sounds soft, but it is strategically powerful. A table that likes the dealer is a table that does not test the dealer. Every dealer has a different personality they bring \u2014 some are witty, some are motherly, some are studious and unflappable \u2014 and the best dealers lean into their natural strengths. The goal is not to be everyone\u2019s friend; it is to project competence and approachability so that players see the dealer as an authority worth cooperating with rather than an obstacle to challenge."));

body.push(h2("10.7 Preventative Dealing and Confrontation Management"));
body.push(para("Preventative dealing is the practice of stopping problems before they escalate, rather than reacting to them after they\u2019ve blown up. The core principle: stay ahead of confrontation between players. When the dealer hears voices rising in tone, even if no specific rule has been broken yet, the dealer steps in immediately: \u2018Guys, you got to stop.\u2019 Serious tone. No exceptions. A table where the dealer consistently intervenes early will have dramatically fewer serious confrontations than a table where the dealer waits for things to get bad. Most physical altercations at poker tables (which do happen) could have been prevented by a dealer who stepped in 30 seconds earlier."));
body.push(para("Confrontation between a player and the dealer: players will test new dealers. A common angle: a player asks a question directed at another player to extract information (e.g., \u2018That\u2019s 20, right?\u2019 when the bet is clearly 15). If the dealer answers, the player may snap \u2018I wasn\u2019t asking you.\u2019 The instinct-driven response: the dealer does not engage verbally. Instead, the dealer spreads the bet chips clearly on the table so the amount is visible. This uses the fundamental rule (all players deserve equal access to table information) as a shield. The player has nothing to argue with. The dealer who handles this smoothly signals to the entire table that they cannot be rattled, which prevents future challenges."));
body.push(para("Critical rules for confrontation: (1) Never give an opinion \u2014 give a clear action or statement using the rules as tools. (2) Never tell a player to \u2018calm down\u2019 \u2014 this escalates almost every time. Use factual statements: \u2018I think that\u2019s over the line\u2019 or \u2018Guys, you\u2019ve got to stop.\u2019 (3) If a player resists the dealer\u2019s ruling, the dealer calls the floor immediately rather than arguing. (4) Any situation involving racism, sexism, or illegal behavior: the dealer must address it every time, even if the room\u2019s culture is to let things go. (5) Intoxicated players who are too impaired to function: the dealer notifies the supervisor immediately. A quiet \u2018Floor\u2019 followed by \u2018seat four\u2019 when the supervisor arrives is sufficient."));

body.push(h2("10.8 Speed Management for New Dealers"));
body.push(para("The number one mistake new dealers make is trying to go too fast. Speed is the visible marker of experience that new dealers try to imitate, but rushing causes errors, lost control, and a table that has no confidence in the dealer. The correct approach: go at whatever pace allows 100% accuracy. Count bets clearly and slowly: \u2018One... two... three hundred... three fifty-five.\u2019 Each stack is separated and visible. Players would rather watch a slow but accurate countdown than a fast dealer who makes mistakes. Speed improves naturally over the first 6-8 months of dealing \u2014 experienced dealers who started slow report that they eventually cash out 30-40% more in tips than when they started, purely from dealing more hands per hour."));
body.push(para("A related trap: letting players tell the dealer what to do. New dealers, especially younger ones, sometimes defer to experienced players who offer to \u2018help\u2019 with chip counting or rule questions. This creates a dependency where the dealer looks to specific players for guidance. The dealer must always be the one to figure things out, even if it takes longer. Players are angrier about errors than about deliberate pace."));

body.push(h2("10.9 Table Surface and Device Policies"));
body.push(para("The felt surface is sacred. Nothing larger than an individual playing card should be placed flat on the felt \u2014 this includes purses, bags, food containers, and chip racks. Large objects can conceal cards, and the dealer must be able to see every card and chip on the table at all times. Chip racks may rest half-on/half-off the rail but should not be placed flat on the felt."));
body.push(para("Cell phone policies vary by room and are evolving rapidly. The general direction: tournaments should prohibit any physical interaction with a phone while a player has cards (headphones are acceptable; looking at or touching the screen is not). Cash games are more permissive \u2014 players may interact with devices when not in the middle of a decision, but the casino reserves the right to restrict a specific player if their device use creates concerns. Recording devices are technically prohibited in most casinos, though enforcement is inconsistent. The dealer should know their room\u2019s specific policy and enforce it consistently."));

body.push(h2("10.10 Poker Room Hierarchy and When to Call the Floor"));
body.push(para("The standard poker room hierarchy from top to bottom: Poker Director/Manager (oversees the entire room, reports to table games management or GM) \u2192 Shift Managers (responsible for everything during their shift; there must always be one on property) \u2192 Supervisors/Floor Staff (handle rulings, player disputes, and table management on the floor) \u2192 Dealers. When the dealer encounters a situation they cannot resolve, they call the supervisor. If the supervisor cannot resolve it, they escalate to the shift manager. If the shift manager is stuck, they go to the director."));
body.push(para("When to call the floor as a dealer: (1) Any premature card \u2014 no exceptions. (2) Any situation where more than one player acted out of turn. (3) Any ambiguity about what a player said or did. (4) Any confrontation that the dealer cannot defuse with a single statement. (5) Any suspected integrity issue (player taking chips back, reaching into the muck, concealing cards inappropriately). (6) Any intoxicated player. (7) Any time the dealer does not know the correct ruling. The dealer should never be afraid to call the floor \u2014 it is explicitly part of the job. However, the dealer should also never tell the players what the floor will decide. The correct communication is always: \u2018I\u2019m calling the floor. They\u2019ll make a decision.\u2019"));
body.push(para("A systemic industry problem: supervisor quality is highly variable because there is no universal supervisor training. Supervisors are typically promoted dealers who learn on the job. Some are excellent; others are inconsistent. Dealers sometimes avoid calling the floor because they do not trust the supervisor to make the right call \u2014 this is a serious integrity failure that the dealer must not participate in. Call the floor regardless of your confidence in the supervisor. The alternative (the dealer making a ruling they are not authorized to make) is always worse."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "A robotic system eliminates the soft-skill challenges of running the game but must replicate the information-management aspects. Must clearly communicate all bet amounts (visual and/or audio display). Must enforce table surface policies (detect and flag objects on the felt that could conceal cards). Must provide a clear escalation pathway for situations requiring human judgment (supervisor notification system). Must maintain consistent pace regardless of game complexity. Must enforce device policies per configurable room rules. The human elements of running the game \u2014 personality, confrontation de-escalation, player psychology \u2014 are the aspects most difficult to replicate robotically and represent the strongest argument for retaining human floor staff alongside robotic dealing systems."));

body.push(h2("10.11 Cross-Firing"));
body.push(para("Cross-firing is the term for dealers at separate tables yelling back and forth to each other. Under no circumstances are dealers allowed to speak to a dealer at another table \u2014 even if both games are dead spreads with no players. Dealers may only communicate with people at their own table or standing directly next to it. Cross-firing is wildly unprofessional and is treated as a disciplinary issue in every serious poker room. It is a casino-wide rule, not just a poker room rule. Supervisors will walk across the room to shut it down, and repeat offenders receive write-ups."));

body.push(h2("10.12 Never Argue with a Guest"));
body.push(para("This principle cannot be overstated: under no circumstances should a dealer argue with a guest. This applies even when the dealer is enforcing rules. Even when the player is objectively wrong. Even when the player is being unreasonable. The dealer\u2019s job is not to win arguments \u2014 it is to manage the game and call the floor when the situation exceeds their authority. Any resistance from a player should result in an immediate floor call. The dealer who argues back, even politely, has already lost. The correct response to any escalation is a calm, factual statement followed by \u2018I\u2019m going to call the floor.\u2019 This is not weakness \u2014 it is the professionally correct action."));

body.push(h2("10.13 Never Give Advice on Play"));
body.push(para("Dealers must never tell a player how to play their hand or give any advice under any circumstances. This includes suggestions, hints, reactions, or commentary about a player\u2019s strategy. The procedure manual is explicit: dealers are not to make any remarks concerning a player\u2019s winnings, method of play, manner of betting (other than procedural instruction), individual level of skill, or luck. This extends to seemingly harmless comments like \u2018good luck all-in\u2019 \u2014 which is considered unprofessional because it shows favoritism toward the all-in player over their opponents. In professional circuit events (WPT, WSOP), saying \u2018good luck all-in\u2019 is treated as a disqualifying marker of an unprofessional dealer. Dealers who are also poker players must be especially careful, as the instinct to comment on play carries over from their player experience."));

body.push(h2("10.14 Rabbit Hunting"));
body.push(para("Rabbit hunting is the practice of revealing what the next community card(s) would have been after a hand ends early. Under no circumstances is this permitted in a casino environment, despite what some dealers do in home games or informal settings. The casino\u2019s position is clear: every hand dealt per hour generates rake (in cash games) or advances the tournament. Rabbit hunting wastes time. Once one player is allowed to see what would have come, every player at the table will request it on future hands, creating a cascading slowdown that measurably reduces hands per hour. The rabbit hunting prohibition is also a game integrity issue: revealing undealt cards provides information about deck composition that could theoretically influence future play in the same session."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must never reveal undealt cards under any circumstances. After a hand concludes, all remaining deck cards must be immediately collected and shuffled without exposure. This is trivially enforced by a robotic system since the undealt cards never need to be in a visible position."));

body.push(h2("10.15 Foul Hands and Unprotected Cards"));
body.push(para("A hand is considered foul if the player\u2019s cards come into contact with the muck in any way. If cards have never been tabled (shown) and they touch the muck \u2014 even slightly \u2014 they are expected to be fouled. If a player attempts to retrieve fouled cards, the dealer must call the floor. The cards are never given back by the dealer on their own authority."));
body.push(para("Unprotected hands create a related problem. If an adjacent player accidentally folds and their discarded cards land on or across another player\u2019s unprotected cards, the contacted hand may be killed. This is always a floor call. The player whose hand was contacted will instinctively protest (\u2018I can tell you what my cards are\u2019), but the integrity concern is that the cards could have been switched or contaminated. Every player must have an opportunity to protect their cards from the initial pitch. If a dealer pitches cards and a player has their back turned, and another player grabs those cards before the intended recipient can protect them, the house typically rules in favor of a misdeal because the player never had a chance to protect their hand."));
body.push(para("Players who deliberately foul their own hand or another player\u2019s hand are a different category entirely. If a player appears to intentionally flick a pitched card up (perhaps because they saw a low card coming from a dealer who is inadvertently flashing), this is not a simple procedural matter \u2014 the dealer must notify a supervisor. Deliberate fouling is a game integrity violation."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must track the physical position of all cards at all times. Must detect when any card contacts the muck pile and flag it as potentially fouled. Must ensure every player\u2019s cards are dealt to a protected position where retrieval by adjacent players is minimized. A robotic system with precise card placement eliminates the majority of accidental foul scenarios."));

body.push(h2("10.16 Player Responsibility to Speak Up"));
body.push(para("The procedure manual places explicit responsibility on players to speak up when they observe errors. This includes situations such as: a player being all-in with a main pot that the dealer forgets to award at showdown, incorrect bet amounts that go unchallenged, dealing errors that no one mentions, or any procedural mistake. A player who deliberately stays silent about a mistake that benefits them can have that silence used against them in a floor ruling. This responsibility exists because game integrity is a shared obligation \u2014 the dealer is the primary guardian, but the players are expected to contribute."));
body.push(para("A specific example from the procedure manual: if a player is all-in on the flop and two remaining players battle in a side pot, and the side pot winner forgets about the main pot and tosses their cards in, the all-in player has the responsibility to speak up and say \u2018there\u2019s a main pot.\u2019 Not every room enforces this identically, but the principle that players share responsibility for game accuracy is widely accepted."));

body.push(h2("10.17 Collusion Detection and Prevention"));
body.push(para("The house has the right to investigate and the right to prevent particular players from playing in the same game. If the casino believes players are working together for any reason, they can separate them into different games, restrict their play, or ban them. Shift managers (not regular supervisors) have the authority to request to see a player\u2019s hand if they suspect collusion, though this power is exercised extremely rarely. The dealer\u2019s role in collusion prevention is primarily observational: watching for patterns of soft play (players who never bet against each other), chip dumping (deliberate losing to transfer chips), and communication between players during hands. When the dealer suspects collusion, they report it to the supervisor \u2014 they do not confront the players or make accusations."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must track betting patterns across sessions to flag potential collusion indicators: players who consistently avoid confrontation with each other, unusual chip transfers, coordinated action patterns. A robotic system with complete hand history data is vastly superior to human observation for detecting statistical anomalies that suggest collusion."));

body.push(h2("10.18 English-Only Rule"));
body.push(para("Almost every poker room designates one official language for table play. In the United States, this is English. Other languages are generally prohibited at the table during active play. The reason is directly tied to the one-player-per-hand rule: if two players are conversing in a language that other players cannot understand, there is no way to verify that they are not discussing the hand, sharing card information, or coordinating action. The English-only rule gives every player confidence that all table communication is transparent. The dealer must enforce this rule when violations occur during a hand, though casual conversation between hands in other languages is typically tolerated. International tournament series may designate a different official language depending on the venue."));

body.push(h2("10.19 Spectator Management"));
body.push(para("Spectators are permitted at the table as a courtesy and are in no way allowed to disrupt, distract, or influence the play of the game. A player has the right to request that the house reposition a spectator who may be able to see their hole cards. If that means the spectator must move or leave the area, so be it. Spectators may never sit in an empty seat at the playing table, even if the game is short-handed with multiple vacant chairs. The house has the right to insist that a spectator leave the immediate area of the table entirely. In tournaments, spectators are generally not permitted in the playing area at all until the final table, where designated spectator areas are established. The dealer should be aware of spectators positioned behind players and proactively ensure no hole card information is being compromised."));

body.push(h2("10.20 Never Look at Face-Down Cards"));
body.push(para("A dealer must never look at any face-down cards under any circumstances. This includes mucked cards, undealt cards in the stub, and any cards that a player has folded. The dealer does not turn up face-down cards out of curiosity, does not peek at what was mucked, and does not examine the stub to see what would have come. This is not merely a rule \u2014 it is a fundamental principle of dealing integrity. The only time a dealer reads cards is when they are tabled (turned face-up) at showdown by the player or per the rules of the game. Even if a card accidentally flips face-up during the muck, the dealer does not draw attention to it beyond what is required by the exposed card procedure."));

body.push(h2("10.21 Deck Position and Handling"));
body.push(para("At all times during play, the deck must be held parallel to the felt and as close to the table surface as possible. Angling the deck \u2014 tilting it upward, rolling it to the side, or holding it high off the felt \u2014 creates the possibility that players can see the bottom card or the upcoming card. This is both an integrity issue and a professionalism marker. An angled deck can result in a card falling off the top, potentially landing on the muck (which creates a misdeal scenario and a floor call). Professional dealers develop the habit of keeping the deck flat and low instinctively, but new dealers frequently let the deck angle upward, especially during complex betting situations when their attention is on the action rather than their grip."));

body.push(h2("10.22 Dead Spreads"));
body.push(para("A dead spread is a table where a dealer sits with no players, the deck suited and spread face-up, waiting for a game to potentially open. Dead spreads are one of the most emotionally charged aspects of the dealing profession because dealers make no tips during a dead spread. However, dead spreads are an essential operational tool for poker rooms. When player traffic increases, the room needs to be able to open a new table immediately. Without a dealer already positioned, the room must wait for the next push rotation to staff the table \u2014 potentially 10 to 30 minutes. Dead spreads allow instant game openings that capture revenue the room would otherwise lose."));
body.push(para("Dealers who complain about dead spreads or confront management about being assigned to them damage their professional reputation. The relationship between dealers and supervisors involves an inherent power imbalance: supervisors control the rotation, table assignments, and scheduling. Dealers who accept dead spreads gracefully and use the time productively (reviewing procedures, resting, chatting with players who sit down) earn respect from supervisors. Dealers who resist them create friction that affects their future assignments, shift preferences, and promotion prospects. Dead spreads are also an opportunity: players occasionally sit at a dead spread table to chat, and these interactions can build relationships that lead to better tips when the game eventually opens."));

body.push(h2("10.23 Panhandling Awareness"));
body.push(para("If a dealer observes a person asking other players for money at the table or in the poker room, they must notify a supervisor immediately. Panhandling creates an extremely uncomfortable environment for players \u2014 some people have difficulty saying no, and the pressure of being asked for money in a public setting can cause a player to never return. The notification must be handled discreetly: the dealer should wait until the panhandler has moved away from the table, then call the floor and identify the person. Some panhandlers are sophisticated about it, asking players to meet them outside or framing the request as borrowing. If the dealer hears any version of a player soliciting money from another player, the supervisor needs to know."));

body.push(h2("10.24 Troublemaker Notification"));
body.push(para("When a dealer recognizes a player who has been previously banned, previously ejected, or who has a known history of causing problems, the dealer should proactively notify the supervisor before the situation escalates. The notification should happen as soon as the dealer sees the person enter the room \u2014 not after they have sat down and started causing problems. The procedure is simple: call the floor (\u2018Floor on six\u2019), and when the supervisor arrives, quietly identify the individual. This preemptive communication prevents incidents before they occur and demonstrates the kind of situational awareness that supervisors value in their dealers."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "A robotic system would handle many of these table management functions differently. Cross-firing, arguing, and giving advice are non-issues for a machine. Rabbit hunting is trivially prevented. Card tracking eliminates foul hand ambiguity. Language detection could flag potential English-only violations. Spectator detection via cameras could identify when someone is positioned to see hole cards. The system must enforce deck-parallel orientation mechanically. Dead spread functionality would be a low-power standby mode. Player identification systems could flag banned or problematic individuals automatically. The human judgment elements \u2014 panhandling awareness, reading social dynamics, making discretionary floor calls \u2014 would require human floor staff working alongside the robotic dealer."));

// ── 11. TOURNAMENT PROCEDURES ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("11. Tournament-Specific Procedures"));
body.push(para("Tournament dealing introduces an entire set of procedures that do not exist in cash games. Tournaments are so different from cash games that they might as well be called another game entirely. The chips have no cash value. Players cannot leave with their chips. The blinds escalate on a timer. Players can receive penalties for rule violations. Every stack gets dealt to whether the player is seated or not. There is no rake to track \u2014 instead, the dealer signs down cards to ensure proper tip pool distribution. These differences are critical for any system deployed at events like the World Series of Poker, which runs over 100 bracelet events each summer."));

body.push(h2("11.1 Tournament Structure and How Players Enter"));
body.push(para("A poker tournament is an event where players pay a fixed buy-in to receive a set number of chips with no cash value. The entry fee is split between the prize pool, the house fee, and sometimes a staffing fee that goes to the dealer tip pool. Players compete until they run out of chips or until a final winner is determined, with prize money distributed to the top 7\u201320% of the field. First place receives the largest share, with decreasing amounts down to the minimum cash (typically 1\u20132x the buy-in)."));
body.push(para("Player entry procedure: In most professional poker rooms, players buy in at a cashier and receive a ticket showing their table and seat number. The ticket has copies for the player, the cashier, and the dealer. When the player arrives at the table, they present the ticket to the dealer. The dealer MUST verify the table and seat number on the ticket \u2014 do not trust players to sit in the correct seat. This is a common error: players frequently sit in wrong seats, and catching it early prevents major disruptions later. After verification, the dealer gives the player a pre-built starting stack (large denomination chips on the bottom) and greets them. It is the player\u2019s responsibility to count the stack."));
body.push(para("Starting stacks are prepared ahead of time, either by dealers during dead spreads or by staff before the tournament begins. Getting all stacks correct is critical \u2014 corrections after the tournament has started create major headaches. Typical starting stacks use 100-unit, 500-unit, and 1,000-unit chips, sometimes with 5,000-unit chips for deeper structures. A common starting stack is 10,000 units."));
body.push(para("In rooms using tournament management software (like Bravo or Poker Atlas), the system controls which seats are available. The poker room typically opens a limited number of seats per table initially (e.g., five of ten seats), sells them randomly, then progressively releases additional seats as the field grows. This prevents tables from being half-empty while others overflow."));

body.push(h2("11.2 Dealing to Empty Seats and Absent Players"));
body.push(para("In tournaments, every stack that is in play gets dealt to, regardless of whether the player is physically present. This is fundamentally different from cash games, where only seated players receive cards. If a player leaves the table temporarily (bathroom, food, phone call), the dealer continues to deal them in. Their blinds and antes are posted from their stack by the adjacent player or the dealer."));
body.push(para("A player\u2019s hand is killed if they are not at their seat when the last card is dealt to all players on the initial deal (TDA Rule 30). \u2018At your seat\u2019 means within arm\u2019s reach of your chair \u2014 not standing across the room, not walking toward the table. If a player is not present, the dealer deals their cards slightly closer to themselves (not all the way to the empty seat) so the cards can be easily mucked after the deal is complete. Kill the absent player\u2019s cards immediately after the last card is dealt to the button \u2014 do not wait until it is that player\u2019s turn to act."));
body.push(para("Close calls on whether a player made it back in time are supervisor decisions, not dealer decisions. If there is any ambiguity, call the floor. The culture of the room matters here: some rooms are strict (the moment the last card leaves the deck, time is up), while others are more lenient. Follow the room\u2019s culture, but default to the rule if unsure."));

body.push(h2("11.3 The Tournament Clock and Blind Levels"));
body.push(para("Tournaments use a visible clock (typically displayed on a television screen via software like Bravo) that counts down the time remaining in each blind level. When the clock reaches zero, the blinds increase for the next hand dealt. The tournament director announces \u2018blinds up\u2019 and states the new blind amounts. The dealer must repeat this clearly: \u2018200/400 blinds, next hand.\u2019"));
body.push(para("The critical timing rule for dealers: if you get the first riffle of your shuffle completed before the clock hits zero, that hand is dealt at the current blind level. If you have not started the riffle when the clock expires, the next hand uses the new higher blinds. Some rooms use the button on the shuffle machine instead of the riffle as the trigger. Know your room\u2019s specific rule, but the riffle is the most common standard."));
body.push(para("Blind structures typically start low relative to starting stacks (e.g., 100/200 with 10,000 starting chips) and escalate every 15\u201330 minutes. A typical progression might be: 100/200, 200/400, 300/600, 400/800, 500/1000, 600/1200, 800/1600, and so on. The increasing blinds force action and ensure players are eliminated over time \u2014 if you do not play hands, the blinds will consume your stack."));

body.push(h2("11.4 Antes: Traditional and Big Blind Ante"));
body.push(h3("Traditional Antes"));
body.push(para("In the traditional ante format, every player at the table posts a fixed ante amount before each hand in addition to the blinds. For example, at the 100/200 level with a 25-unit ante, all nine players post 25 units each (225 total) before the hand begins. The dealer must collect all antes before cutting the deck, then pitch the cards. The challenge: collecting antes from every player takes significant time, and dealers must constantly remind players to post (\u2018Come on, put the antes out\u2019). This is the slower, older format."));
body.push(h3("Big Blind Ante (Modern Standard)"));
body.push(para("The big blind ante format, adopted widely since approximately 2017, eliminates individual antes. Instead, the player in the big blind posts both the big blind AND an ante equal to approximately one big blind. So at 200/400 with a big blind ante, the big blind player posts 400 (ante) plus 400 (big blind) = 800 total. This is dramatically faster because only one player posts the ante instead of nine. The dealer collects the big blind ante into the center before pitching \u2014 it goes in with the blinds as part of the pot. Most professional tournament series (WSOP, WPT) now use this format."));
body.push(para("Key rule for short stacks with big blind ante: if a player in the big blind has fewer chips than the combined ante and big blind, the ante is posted first. So if a player has 400 chips and the structure is 200/400 with a 400 big blind ante, the entire 400 goes to the ante. There is no big blind posted. If that player wins the hand, they only win the antes. If the player has 500, then 400 goes to the ante and 100 to the big blind \u2014 they can only win 100 from each player\u2019s bet beyond the antes."));

body.push(h2("11.5 Seating Cards and Player Tracking"));
body.push(para("Every tournament table has seating cards \u2014 laminated cards showing the table number and seat number (e.g., \u2018Table 4, Seat 10\u2019). These are kept in order in the dealer\u2019s well. When a player is eliminated, the dealer immediately places the corresponding seating card in the empty seat, visible to supervisors. This is how the tournament staff tracks which seats are open for reseating, table balancing, and table breaks."));
body.push(para("Keeping seating cards in order is critical. One of the most common and most frustrating dealer mistakes in tournaments is forgetting to put the seating card out when a player is eliminated, or having the cards jumbled out of order in the well. When a supervisor needs to find a specific open seat and the cards are out of order, it creates a cascading delay. Train yourself: the moment a player\u2019s last chips are pushed to the winner, find that seat\u2019s card and place it. If you tap into a tournament table, check the seating cards within your first few hands to verify they match the actual empty seats."));
body.push(para("When a player arrives at the table with a seating card (from an alternate list or a broken table), verify the table and seat number, take the card back, and place it in order in your well. This is where cards get out of order \u2014 the incoming card must go back in sequence. Take the extra second to do it right."));
body.push(h3("Announcing Eliminations"));
body.push(para("When a player is eliminated, some rooms require the dealer to yell \u2018seat open.\u2019 Others use \u2018player down.\u2019 Both can feel harsh to the player who just lost. The more professional approach: place the seating card, and after the player has left the area, call the supervisor\u2019s name at a normal volume and say \u2018seat open.\u2019 For example: \u2018Christy, seat open.\u2019 This gives the eliminated player dignity while still communicating the information. If multiple tables exist, seating cards need to be collected frequently by tournament staff."));
body.push(para("Seating cards are only necessary when there are three or more tables in a tournament. When the field is down to two tables, supervisors are directly watching both tables and seating cards become unnecessary. Some rooms still use them out of habit; follow your room\u2019s procedure."));

body.push(h2("11.6 Re-Entry, Rebuy, and Add-On"));
body.push(para("Modern tournaments commonly allow re-entry: if a player is eliminated during the re-entry period (which can last hours after the start), they can go back to the cashier, purchase a new ticket, and receive a new random table and seat assignment. Re-entry generates an additional entry fee for the house, which is why casinos prefer it. The player gets a completely fresh starting stack at a new seat."));
body.push(para("Rebuy tournaments are different: the player stays in their same seat and purchases additional chips at the table. The dealer handles the cash transaction, counts the money, and places it in the well. Rebuy chips (often in a different denomination, like 5,000-unit chips) are given directly by the dealer. Rebuys are typically only allowed when a player\u2019s stack is below a certain threshold (sometimes including immediately after buying in). 100% of rebuy money typically goes to the prize pool, unlike re-entries where the house takes a fee."));
body.push(para("Add-ons are one-time purchases offered to all remaining players at a specific point in the tournament, usually during the first break after the rebuy period ends. Everyone is offered the chance to buy additional chips for a fixed price. The dealer collects the money and distributes the chips. Some tournaments have a dealer add-on where the money goes to the dealer tip pool rather than the prize pool."));

body.push(h2("11.7 Button Assignment and Positioning"));
body.push(para("At the start of a tournament, one table deals for button. The dealer shuffles, cuts, and deals one card face-up \u2014 whichever seat gets the highest card, that\u2019s where the button starts. The supervisor communicates the starting seat to all tables: \u2018Button starts in seat 5.\u2019 Every table in the tournament uses the same starting seat for fairness. If an 11th table opens later, the button starts in the same seat."));
body.push(para("Tournament play uses a dead button (TDA standard). This means the button can sit in an empty seat. The key implications: a player who sits down in a dead button seat gets dealt in immediately in most modern tournament rooms. A player arriving between the button and big blind can take the small blind position. A player arriving between the button and small blind must wait. Players cannot buy the button in tournaments (unlike some cash game rules). The dead button rule exists because tournament players have no control over where they are seated \u2014 if their previous table broke and they land in the big blind twice in a row, that\u2019s an accepted consequence of tournament structure."));
body.push(para("Dealing starts when four or more players are seated (most rooms). Three or fewer players at a table means the dealer waits until more entries arrive."));

body.push(h2("11.8 Color-Up and Race-Off"));
body.push(para("As blind levels increase, lower denomination chips are removed from play. The dealer must exchange (color up) small chips for larger ones. The procedure: one player at the table agrees to buy up all the small denomination chips from the other players. The dealer goes around the table, taking the small chips from each player and giving them equivalent larger chips, making change as needed."));
body.push(para("For example, if coloring up green (25-unit) chips to black (100-unit) chips: a player with 275 in green gets 200 exchanged for two black chips, with 75 remaining. A player with 125 gets 100 exchanged for one black chip, with 25 remaining. The player who volunteered to buy them up receives all the collected small chips, which the supervisor then exchanges for equivalent larger denomination chips."));
body.push(para("This must be done very deliberately and carefully. You are touching players\u2019 stacks \u2014 surveillance may need to review the footage. Be clear with every transaction. Announce each exchange verbally."));
body.push(h3("The Race-Off"));
body.push(para("After the buy-up, players with remaining odd chips that cannot be evenly exchanged participate in a race-off. Each odd chip entitles the player to one card dealt face-up. The dealer shuffles a fresh deck and deals one card per chip, stair-stepping them so each card is visible. Before dealing, verify that no stray chips were missed \u2014 once cards are dealt, there is no going back."));
body.push(para("Collect all the odd chips into a central pile. Count them to determine how many replacement chips to distribute. Most rooms round up: if there are 275 in odd chips and the replacement denomination is 100, three replacement chips are given out (rounding up from 2.75). The highest card wins the first chip, next highest gets the second, and so on. If a player wins two cards, they only win one replacement chip \u2014 a player can only win once. Ties are broken by suit: spades (highest), hearts, diamonds, clubs (lowest) \u2014 reverse alphabetical order. After distribution, remove the dealt race-off cards and resume tournament play."));

body.push(h2("11.9 Table Breaks"));
body.push(para("When enough players have been eliminated, the tournament director breaks a table by moving its remaining players to fill empty seats at other tables. The dealer finishes the current hand, then the process begins."));
body.push(para("The supervisor comes to the table with randomized seating cards from empty seats at other tables. The dealer deals one card face-up to each remaining player (starting position varies by room \u2014 some start at the button, others go by seat order). The player with the highest card gets the first seating assignment. The supervisor places a randomized seating card in front of that player, then proceeds clockwise. Each player racks their chips and takes them to their new table with their seating card."));
body.push(para("Players must transport chips in racks in most rooms. Players may NOT carry loose chips in pockets or hands \u2014 this takes chips out of view and can result in forfeiture (TDA Rule 63). If you see a player pocketing chips, you must say something immediately. The forfeited chips are removed from the tournament."));
body.push(para("When a player arrives at your table from a broken table, they will have a seating card. Verify the table and seat number. Place their card back in order in your well. Do not skip this step \u2014 it is exactly how seating cards get out of order."));
body.push(h3("Table Balancing"));
body.push(para("With three or more tables, the tournament keeps tables balanced within two players of each other. If one table has eight players and others have six, the tournament does not move anyone \u2014 players get eliminated quickly enough that it will even out. Attempting to balance within one player at this stage would create constant disruption. However, when only two tables remain, they are kept balanced within one player. When a player needs to move for balancing, the next player due for the big blind is moved, and they receive the worst available position (closest to the big blind) at the destination table. The supervisor handles the selection and communication."));

body.push(h2("11.10 Bounty Tournaments"));
body.push(para("In a bounty tournament, a portion of each player\u2019s buy-in is designated as a bounty. For example, in a $250 buy-in with a $100 bounty, $150 goes to the prize pool and $100 is the bounty. Every player receives a bounty chip in addition to their playing stack. Whoever eliminates a player wins their bounty chip, which is redeemed for cash."));
body.push(para("Key rule: if a player pushes their bounty chip into the pot, they are declaring all-in. The dealer announces all-in and places the all-in button. The bounty chip itself does not have a playing value \u2014 it is a marker for the cash bounty. When a player is eliminated, their bounty chip goes to the player who knocked them out. If two players split the knockout (side pot scenario where one player wins the main pot and eliminates the short stack), the bounty is split between them. If only one player\u2019s pot eliminated the player, only that player wins the bounty."));
body.push(para("Bounty redemption varies by room: some rooms have the dealer pay out immediately in cash chips, others have supervisors collect bounty chips periodically and pay the players. Bounty tournaments encourage players to show up on time (they want to collect bounties) and change the strategic dynamics of play (players are more willing to call with marginal hands to chase a bounty)."));

body.push(h2("11.11 Breaks and Dealer Responsibilities"));
body.push(para("Tournament breaks are typically 5\u201315 minutes and occur at the end of a blind level. The tournament clock displays \u2018BREAK\u2019 with a countdown. During breaks, players are often not allowed near the tournament tables. Dealer responsibilities during breaks: (1) Suit the deck \u2014 spread all cards face-up and organized by suit so players can verify all 52 cards are present when they return. (2) Verify seating cards match actual empty seats. (3) Sign the down card if not already done. (4) Prepare for the next level (know the new blind amounts). (5) Handle any color-up or race-off if scheduled for this break."));
body.push(para("Suiting a deck during a break: the simplest method is sorting into four piles by suit, then ordering each suit king-through-ace. A faster method is to separate red and black cards first, then sort each pile. An experienced dealer can suit a deck in under a minute. The suited deck is left face-up and spread on the table for player inspection when they return."));
body.push(para("The down card is a tracking system \u2014 usually an index card at each table where every dealer who sits in the box signs their name. This is the only way to ensure proper tournament tip pool distribution. Each \u2018down\u2019 (typically 30 minutes at a table) earns the dealer a share of the tournament tip pool. If you forget to sign, you may not get paid for that down. Sign immediately when you sit down, before dealing your first hand."));

body.push(h2("11.12 Penalties"));
body.push(para("Tournaments differ fundamentally from cash games in that players can receive penalties for rule violations. In a cash game, there is little leverage over a misbehaving player beyond asking them to leave (and they take their money). In a tournament, missing hands due to a penalty means the blinds and antes eat your stack while you cannot play \u2014 a devastating punishment."));
body.push(para("Penalty structure (TDA Rule 71): penalties escalate from verbal warnings to missed-hand penalties to disqualification. A one-round penalty means the player misses one hand per player at the table (including themselves). A second offense doubles the penalty. Third offense triples it. Repeat infractions may lead to disqualification. The dealer tracks where the button was when the penalty began \u2014 the player returns when the button returns to that position."));
body.push(para("Common penalty triggers: (1) Exposing cards while action is pending. (2) Soft play \u2014 checking the absolute nuts last to act (e.g., four of a kind when the board makes it the best possible hand). Why would a player not bet the best hand? The only reason is collusion or angle-shooting. (3) Speaking a language other than the table\u2019s designated language. (4) Repeated action out of turn. (5) Disruptive behavior, cursing, or belligerence. (6) Persistent delay of game. (7) Touching another player\u2019s person, cards, or chips. (8) Poor chip visibility."));
body.push(para("As a dealer, you never issue penalties yourself \u2014 you call the floor. But you must recognize penalty-worthy behavior and report it. If a player exposes their hand to another player during a hand, that hand is set aside and the supervisor is called. The hand may be exposed to the table at the end of the hand, and the offending player may receive a penalty. Do not let violations slide because you like the player \u2014 other players notice inconsistency and it destroys trust."));

body.push(h2("11.13 Player Responsibilities (TDA Rule 2)"));
body.push(para("Tournament rules place significant responsibility on players, which is enforced via the penalty system. Players are expected to: verify their registration and seat assignment, protect their hands, make intentions clear, follow the action, act in turn with proper terminology and gestures, defend their right to act, keep cards visible and chips correctly stacked (clean stacks of 20, large denominations visible), remain at the table with a live hand, table all cards at showdown, speak up if they see a mistake, call for a clock when warranted, transfer tables promptly, follow the one-player-to-a-hand rule, and generally contribute to an orderly event."));
body.push(para("The \u2018speak up if they see a mistake\u2019 responsibility is particularly important. If a player notices the dealer miscounted a bet or dealt an incorrect number of cards, they are expected to say something. A player who deliberately stays silent about a mistake that benefits them can have that silence used against them in a floor ruling. This shifts some burden of game integrity from the dealer to the entire table."));

body.push(h2("11.14 Accepted Action (TDA Rule 49)"));
body.push(para("One of the most critical tournament rules: \u2018Poker is a game of alert, continuous observation. It is the caller\u2019s responsibility to determine the correct amount of an opponent\u2019s bet before calling, regardless of what is stated by others.\u2019 If a dealer miscounts a bet (announces $17 million instead of $22 million, for example), and the opponent calls that stated amount, the caller has accepted the full correct action and is subject to the full correct amount."));
body.push(para("This rule was famously invoked at the 2019 WSOP Main Event with 11 players remaining. The dealer miscounted an all-in, a player called based on the stated amount, and tournament director Jack Effel ruled it as \u2018accepted action\u2019 \u2014 the call stood for the full correct amount. As a dealer, this rule does not get you off the hook for a miscount \u2014 you may still receive a write-up. But it means the game continues correctly despite the error."));

body.push(h2("11.15 Face-Up for All-Ins (TDA Rule 16)"));
body.push(para("When a player is all-in and all betting action by all other players is complete, all hands must be tabled (turned face-up) without delay. No player who is all-in or who has called all betting action may muck their hand without showing. This applies to both main and side pots. As a dealer: (1) If a player goes all-in on the flop and is called, get all hands face-up before dealing the turn. (2) If all action completes on the river with an all-in player, all hands must be shown. (3) Protect the muck \u2014 a player may instinctively try to muck their losing hand. Do not allow it. The hand must be tabled."));

body.push(h2("11.16 Chips Out of Play"));
body.push(para("Tournament integrity depends on every chip being accounted for. Three TDA rules address chips leaving play:"));
body.push(para("All-in with chips found behind (TDA Rule 62): If a player declares all-in and a hidden chip is discovered afterward, the tournament director determines whether it was part of the accepted action. If not, the chip does not count toward the all-in amount \u2014 it is not paid off if the player wins, and it does not save them if they lose. The tournament director may award the chip to the winning caller."));
body.push(para("Chips out of view and in transit (TDA Rule 63): Players may not hold or transport chips in any way that takes them out of view. Violation results in chip forfeiture and possible disqualification. Forfeited chips are removed from tournament play entirely. This is why rooms provide racks for chip transport during table breaks."));
body.push(para("Lost and found chips (TDA Rule 64): Any chip found for which ownership cannot be determined is taken out of play and returned to tournament inventory. As a dealer, if you see a chip on the floor, report it immediately. If you see a player pocket a chip even momentarily, report it immediately."));

body.push(h2("11.17 Chops and End-Game Negotiations"));
body.push(para("When a tournament reaches the later stages (final table or near it), players may negotiate to split the remaining prize money and end the tournament early. This is called a chop. As a dealer: do not participate in the discussion, but be aware of it. If one player says no, the conversation is supposed to end. If other players continue pressuring the player who declined, get a supervisor to the table. Do not allow the table to bully a player into a chop."));
body.push(para("If a chop is agreed upon, all players push their chip stacks forward and may not touch them again. The dealer counts down each player\u2019s stack carefully, announcing each total. The supervisor calculates the distribution (often using ICM \u2014 Independent Chip Model \u2014 which factors in chip counts and prize structure). The dealer\u2019s job is accurate chip counts and clear presentation."));

body.push(h2("11.18 Hand-for-Hand Play"));
body.push(para("Near the money bubble (the point where remaining players begin to receive prize payouts), the tournament enters hand-for-hand play. All tables must start and complete each hand simultaneously. No new hand can begin until all tables have finished. The dealer must signal the floor when their hand is complete and wait for authorization to proceed. This creates coordination overhead that does not exist in normal play. The TDA recommends deducting two minutes from the tournament clock per hand-for-hand deal, rather than pausing the clock entirely (TDA Recommended Procedure 8)."));

body.push(h2("11.19 Making Change in Tournaments"));
body.push(para("Unlike cash games, the dealer\u2019s well in a tournament is typically empty \u2014 there is no bank to make change from. When change is needed (a player bets with a chip larger than the intended bet), the dealer must find another player willing to break the chip. The procedure: slide the chip to the center or hold it with one finger visible on the felt and ask: \u2018Can you make change for this, please?\u2019 Be clear and polite. If no one volunteers, ask the player with the largest stack. Never force a player to make change without asking politely first. Never hold up a chip or point at a player \u2014 use an open hand gesture to direct attention."));

body.push(h2("11.20 Tips in Tournaments"));
body.push(para("Tournament tip handling differs completely from cash games. There are three scenarios: (1) When a player cashes out: they receive a payout slip with a line to designate a dealer tip. This goes into the tournament tip pool, distributed evenly based on downs dealt. (2) During play: if a player tips from a big pot or bounty win, that tip also goes to the tournament pool, not the individual dealer\u2019s box. Taking tournament tips for yourself is treated as seriously as theft. (3) Promotion-related tips: if a player wins a high-hand promotion during a tournament, tips from that specific promotion are typically kept by the individual dealer. Verify this with your supervisor before putting anything in your box."));
body.push(para("Accept every tip graciously regardless of where it goes. Never refuse a tip. Never explain to the player that it doesn\u2019t go to you personally. Say thank you and handle the logistics quietly."));

body.push(h2("11.21 Electronic Devices and Communication (TDA Rule 5)"));
body.push(para("Players may not talk on a phone at the table. Ringtones, music, images, and video must be inaudible and non-disturbing. Betting apps and strategy charts may not be used by players with live hands. Photography, videography, and other devices must not create a nuisance, delay the game, or provide competitive advantage. Violations are subject to penalty. Tournaments enforce these rules much more strictly than cash games."));

body.push(h2("11.22 Final Table Procedures"));
body.push(para("The final table of a major tournament typically involves television cameras, professional commentary, and a card-by-card delay for broadcast. The dealer must handle cards with extra care (RFID-embedded cards that must be placed precisely on the reader), manage the pace to accommodate broadcast requirements, and maintain composure under intense public scrutiny. When breaking from two tables to the final table, all players redraw for seats and a new button position. Poker rooms often assign their strongest dealers to final tables \u2014 this is where any mistake will be seen by thousands of viewers."));
body.push(para("When the final table is reached: the dealer deals for button among all players (one card each, highest card gets the button). This is a complete redraw. The tournament clock pauses during the transition. The supervisor manages the seating, and the dealer focuses on accuracy and composure."));

body.push(h2("11.23 Sit-and-Go Tournaments"));
body.push(para("A sit-and-go is an unscheduled tournament that starts when enough players register (typically 6 or 10 players, one table). There is no tournament clock on a screen \u2014 the dealer uses a timer (often a physical stopwatch or phone timer) and announces blinds up when the timer expires. Sit-and-gos are faster: shorter blind levels (10\u201312 minutes), smaller starting stacks, no re-entry. The same dealer typically deals the entire sit-and-go start to finish, keeping all tips. No seating cards, no table breaks, no alternates. The dealer builds the starting stacks, distributes them, and runs the game."));

body.push(h2("11.24 The Rotation and Table Breaks: Managing Dealer Expectations"));
body.push(para("In poker rooms where cash games and tournaments run simultaneously, dealers rotate through all tables. When a tournament table breaks, the dealer is pulled from the rotation and reassigned to a new position. This commonly results in the dealer missing tables they were expecting to reach \u2014 tables that may tip better or have easier players. This is one of the most emotionally charged issues for dealers in the poker industry."));
body.push(para("The reality: when your tournament table breaks, you may end up anywhere in the rotation. Building an expectation that you will reach a specific table is a mistake that can lead to resentment, poor customer service, and even disciplinary action. Supervisors do their best to place broken-table dealers fairly, but the logistics of managing a rotation with simultaneous cash and tournament play are complex \u2014 too complex for most dealers to fully understand from their position. Nine out of ten times that a dealer believes a supervisor is manipulating the rotation, they are wrong. Do not let rotation frustration affect your professionalism. The one expectation that is always safe: deal every table you sit at as if it is the best table in the room."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "Must track all tournament state: blind levels, antes, player stacks, seating assignments, eliminated players, penalty timers, bounty chip ownership, and clock synchronization across tables. Must enforce TDA rules automatically (face-up for all-ins, dead button, hand killing for absent players, chips-visible requirements). Must handle color-up calculations instantly. Must coordinate hand-for-hand play across multiple tables without human floor staff. Must maintain a complete audit trail of every chip in the tournament from start to finish. Must support multiple tournament formats: freezeout, re-entry, rebuy, bounty, sit-and-go. Tournament dealing is where robotic systems would provide the most dramatic improvement in accuracy and consistency over human dealers."));

// ── 12. REGULATORY CONSIDERATIONS ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("12. Regulatory and Compliance Considerations"));
body.push(para("Any robotic dealing system deployed in a real casino environment must pass regulatory approval from the relevant gaming control board. This section outlines the regulatory landscape based on operator interviews and publicly available regulatory frameworks."));

body.push(h2("12.1 Nevada Gaming Control Board"));
body.push(para("Nevada has the most developed regulatory framework for gaming technology in the United States. New table game technology must be submitted for testing, which includes hardware reliability, software integrity, mathematical fairness verification, and security evaluation. The testing process typically takes 6 to 12 months and costs $50,000 to $200,000 depending on complexity. A robotic dealer would likely be classified as a novel table game system and would face scrutiny on card randomization, game integrity, payout accuracy, and fail-safe behavior."));

body.push(h2("12.2 Game Integrity Requirements"));
body.push(para("A poker dealing system must demonstrate that it cannot be manipulated to favor or disadvantage any player. This means the shuffle must be provably random, the dealing sequence must be deterministic and verifiable, and no system component can access card identity information before the card is dealt. Surveillance integration is mandatory: the system must provide clear camera views of all card movements and chip transactions."));

body.push(h2("12.3 Fail-Safe Requirements"));
body.push(para("If the system malfunctions mid-hand, there must be a defined procedure for completing or voiding the hand. Player chips must be preserved. The system must have redundant tracking so that the game state can be reconstructed after any failure. Battery backup or graceful degradation must be considered for power interruptions."));

body.push(h2("12.4 Virginia Model (Charitable Gaming)"));
body.push(para("The Virginia charitable gaming market provides a detailed case study of what regulatory entry looks like for poker in a new jurisdiction. Virginia\u2019s poker bill (Senate Bill 936) was passed after approximately five years of advocacy, going into effect July 1st of its passage year. The law was built on Virginia\u2019s 50+ year tradition of charitable gaming (primarily bingo), operated by 501(c)(3) organizations, churches, synagogues, VFW posts, Moose and Elks lodges, and similar organizations."));
body.push(para("The poker room director is personally responsible for knowing all regulations and legal requirements. This is not a delegable task. The regulations are extensive and, in Virginia\u2019s case, were entirely new (all underlined in the regulatory document, indicating brand-new language with no prior precedent). The initial regulations tend to be simultaneously restrictive in some areas and unrestricted in others, because the regulators have not yet encountered the edge cases that emerge during actual operation. First-year regulations are expected to be refined repeatedly as issues arise."));
body.push(para("Operational decisions that require regulatory awareness include: chip design and sourcing (chip manufacturers like Icon typically only sell to licensed casinos), table design and layout (including felt color, logo placement, and betting line decisions), chair selection, room layout, staffing requirements, and game approval. Even aesthetic choices like stitching patterns on chairs matter because they affect the overall impression of legitimacy and professionalism that regulators and players expect."));
body.push(para("Each state has different requirements, and a robotic dealing system would need to be certified in each jurisdiction independently. Some jurisdictions (like Macau) require dealers to be local residents, which is part of what drives the labor shortage that creates the opportunity for automation. The Virginia model demonstrates that regulatory entry requires a minimum of 5+ years of advocacy, detailed knowledge of existing charitable gaming frameworks, and a willingness to work through initial regulatory ambiguity."));

body.push(h2("12.5 Dealer Procedure Manuals"));
body.push(para("Every casino provides (or should provide) a dealer procedure manual to new hires. Dealers are 100% responsible for knowing everything in their procedure manual \u2014 there are no exceptions and no excuses. \u2018I didn\u2019t read the manual\u2019 is never an acceptable defense when a procedure is violated. The manual covers game rules, chip handling protocols, security procedures, conduct expectations, and casino-specific policies. Dealers should read the entire manual within their first month of employment, ask questions about anything unclear, and be prepared for the fact that some procedures in the manual may not match actual room practice (manuals are often outdated because the effort to revise wording is substantial, and the people who originally wrote them may no longer work there). Nonetheless, the manual remains the official standard, and the dealer is held to it."));
body.push(para("Procedure manuals also serve as the baseline for disciplinary action. Write-ups, recommendations for promotion, and performance evaluations all reference the manual\u2019s standards. A dealer who invested time in learning the manual during their first month has a foundation that protects them throughout their career. A dealer who skipped it may face consequences months or years later when a situation arises that was covered in the manual they never read."));

body.push(h2("12.6 Attendance Policies and Point Systems"));
body.push(para("Every casino has its own attendance policy, and dealers are notorious for attendance problems. The casino industry requires work during nights, weekends, and holidays \u2014 precisely the times that normal society has off. This creates constant tension between dealers who want to enjoy their personal lives and a business that needs coverage during peak hours. Dealers who call out frequently, arrive late, or leave early develop a stigma that affects every aspect of their career: shift assignments, table rotation quality, part-time vs. full-time status, and promotion eligibility."));
body.push(para("Many casinos use a point system to manage attendance objectively. A typical system works on a rolling 12-month window: a call-out costs one point, being late costs half a point, leaving early costs half to one point depending on how much of the shift has elapsed. Accumulating points triggers escalating consequences: verbal warning at three points, written warning at five, final warning at seven, termination at eight. Points expire one year from the date they were earned. Some rooms are more generous (up to 14 points), but the principle is the same."));
body.push(para("Holiday blackout days add another layer. During major tournament series, holiday weekends, or other high-traffic periods, entire weeks may be blacked out \u2014 meaning call-outs during those periods carry double points or other enhanced penalties. Calling out on Christmas, Thanksgiving, or New Year\u2019s guarantees a reputation problem regardless of the point cost. There is no way for the casino to verify whether the absence is genuine, so every holiday absence is treated with skepticism."));
body.push(para("The smartest approach for dealers is to save their points for genuine emergencies. Dealers who use points early in the year for convenience (beach days, vacations, late nights) find themselves working sick later in the year because they cannot afford additional points. Attendance is consistently cited as the number one factor in promotion decisions \u2014 rooms will not promote a dealer they cannot depend on to show up."));

body.push(h2("12.7 Cell Phone and Badge Policies"));
body.push(para("Dealers cannot have cell phones on the gaming floor. This is a strict rule with no exceptions. If a phone rings in a dealer\u2019s pocket during a hand, it is a write-up. Under no circumstances is it acceptable for a dealer to reach into their pocket and interact with a phone while in the poker room or on the casino floor. Many poker rooms do not provide lockers, so dealers carry their phones in their pockets on silent \u2014 but touching or looking at the phone while on the floor is prohibited. Supervisors sometimes use their phones for operational purposes, which can create the appearance of a double standard, but dealers should not use this as justification for their own phone use."));
body.push(para("Dealer badges must be worn and visible at all times while on the gaming floor. This is consistent across every casino property. Being caught without a visible badge is typically an automatic write-up. The badge serves dual purposes: it identifies the dealer to players and surveillance, and it is part of the security protocol that ensures only authorized personnel are handling gaming equipment and chips."));

body.push(h2("12.8 Aprons"));
body.push(para("Almost every poker room requires dealers to wear an apron while on the gaming floor. The apron is a garment that covers the front of the dealer\u2019s body, theoretically preventing them from reaching into their pockets (where they could conceal stolen chips). In practice, the aprons at most properties do not actually cover the dealer\u2019s pockets \u2014 an inconsistency that has frustrated supervisors for decades. Regardless of whether the apron achieves its ostensible security purpose, the policy is the policy: the apron must be worn at all times on the gaming floor, and not wearing it is a disciplinary issue. It is one of the easiest rules to comply with and one of the most common sources of preventable write-ups."));

body.push(h2("12.9 Moonlighting Restrictions"));
body.push(para("Many poker rooms prohibit moonlighting \u2014 working at another poker room or dealing in any outside capacity while employed. The restriction typically extends beyond competing casinos to include cruise ships, home games, bar poker, and charity events. The casino wants to be the dealer\u2019s primary commitment and does not want to compete with other rooms for the dealer\u2019s time and flexibility. Violating a moonlighting policy is a terminable offense: multiple dealers have been fired for dealing at other rooms, especially when they tried to do it quietly. Getting caught dealing home games, charity events, or bar poker is less common, but the policy typically covers all of these. Dealers should know their room\u2019s specific moonlighting policy before accepting any outside dealing work."));

body.push(h2("12.10 Texas Card Room Regulatory Model"));
body.push(para("Texas operates poker rooms under a regulatory loophole that creates unique dealer procedures. Under Texas regulations, player buy-in money must remain in play until the player cashes out \u2014 it cannot be tipped to the dealer during the game. To work around this, Texas card rooms use \u2018tip chips\u2019: special-denomination chips that players purchase separately at the cashier. These tip chips cannot be wagered in the game, but they can be given to the dealer as gratuity. Players who run out of tip chips can purchase more from other players at the table (the money stays in play, just in a different stack). Players can also tip in cash. This system adds a layer of procedural complexity for dealers who must distinguish between playing chips and tip chips, and must never allow tip chips to enter the pot."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "A robotic system must comply with jurisdiction-specific policies that affect its operation. Cell phone and badge requirements are irrelevant to a machine, but attendance reliability is a major selling point \u2014 a robotic dealer never calls out sick, never arrives late, and never requests holidays off. Apron requirements would not apply. Moonlighting restrictions are irrelevant. The Texas tip chip model would require the system to track two chip categories and prevent tip chips from entering the pot. Procedure manual compliance is inherently perfect for a robotic system since the rules are programmed directly into the software."));

// ── 13. SECURITY AND GAME PROTECTION ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("13. Security and Game Protection"));
body.push(para("Casino poker is a target for cheating, collusion, and manipulation. A robotic dealing system must not only deal accurately but must also incorporate game protection measures that are at least equivalent to what a human dealer provides, and ideally superior."));

body.push(h2("13.1 Anti-Cheating Measures"));
body.push(para("Human dealers are trained to watch for several categories of cheating: marked cards (players who can identify card values from the back), card manipulation (false shuffles, card palming, mucking), chip manipulation (past-posting, which is adding chips to a bet after seeing a favorable card), collusion (two players working together to exploit other players), and mechanical cheating (devices that can read or manipulate cards)."));
body.push(h3("Documented False Shuffle Techniques"));
body.push(para("In a landmark security analysis session organized by TruePokerDealer, professional magicians demonstrated the specific techniques that could theoretically be used by a corrupt dealer to manipulate the deck through the standard casino shuffle sequence. These techniques are documented here not as instructions but as the threat model that a robotic system must be immune to:"));
body.push(para("Retention Shuffle (Blind Shuffle): The dealer performs what appears to be a standard riffle but retains specific cards in known positions (top, bottom, or both) throughout. Cards on the top of the deck can be held in place during the riffle by controlling which half falls last. Cards on the bottom can be retained by controlling the initial split. A skilled manipulator can retain 2-4 cards in position through multiple riffles. The magicians demonstrated retaining cards through two full riffles with the deck appearing to shuffle normally."));
body.push(para("Push-Through Shuffle: The most deceptive false riffle. The two halves are interlaced during the riffle, but instead of being squared together, the halves are pushed through each other and separated again. It appears that the deck is squared and then cut, but the cards have returned to their original positions. This technique naturally leads into a cut (the halves separate into a cut position), making it look like a legitimate riffle followed by a legitimate cut. If the push-through is executed at the correct position, the entire deck retains its order."));
body.push(para("Zarrow Shuffle (named after Herb Zarrow): A false riffle where the two halves appear to interleave but one half slides on top of the other without actually interlacing. The result is a split, an apparent riffle, a squaring, and the deck is in the same order it started. This can be executed in a single motion and is considered the most dangerous false shuffle because it mimics a real shuffle so closely that even trained observers miss it."));
body.push(para("False Box/Strip: Instead of stripping from top to bottom (which reverses the quarter order), the manipulator strips from bottom to top or performs the strip in a way that returns the quarters to their original positions. From the outside, the motion looks identical. Surveillance can detect this if they know to watch the direction of the strip, but most observers cannot distinguish the two at game speed."));
body.push(para("False Cut: A one-handed false cut where the deck appears to be cut but the blocking finger on the bottom retains the card order. Two-handed false cuts are easier to execute but are a red flag (legitimate casino cuts use one hand). The magicians demonstrated one-handed false cuts that were visually indistinguishable from real cuts, though they noted it is the hardest part of the sequence to fake."));
body.push(h3("Documented Peeking Techniques"));
body.push(para("The Bubble Peek: While holding the deck in the dealing position, the dealer applies slight thumb pressure to the top card, creating a tiny \u2018bubble\u2019 (a bend in the card) that briefly exposes the index corner. This is done during normal dealing motion \u2014 while announcing action, looking at the board, or moving chips. The peek takes a fraction of a second. The magicians demonstrated peeking at the top 1-5 cards during what appeared to be normal dealing activity, with the peek completely invisible to players or a camera positioned at table level."));
body.push(para("The Misdirection Peek: The dealer creates a small distraction (chip movement, verbal announcement, reaching for something) and peeks at the top card during the moment when all eyes are directed elsewhere. Combined with second dealing (dealing the second card from the top instead of the first), this allows the dealer to control which player receives specific cards."));
body.push(para("The magicians concluded that a skilled card manipulator who was also a trained dealer could, in theory, stack the deck through the standard casino shuffle sequence if the wash were omitted or poorly executed. The wash is the single procedure that defeats all of these techniques, because it physically separates all cards from their positions before the riffle sequence begins. This is why surveillance enforcement of the wash is considered the highest priority in shuffle monitoring."));
body.push(boldPara("Robotic Advantage: ", "A properly designed robotic system would be immune to all forms of false shuffles, false cuts, peeking, and deck manipulation. The shuffle would be mechanically verifiable (every card position tracked). Card identities would not be accessible to any system component before dealing. The system would never inadvertently expose a card. There would be no possibility of second dealing, bottom dealing, or card retention. This eliminates the entire threat model described above and is a significant security improvement over human dealing."));

body.push(h2("13.2 Surveillance Integration"));
body.push(para("Casino surveillance (the \u201Ceye in the sky\u201D) monitors all table games via overhead cameras. Dealers are trained to perform certain actions in view of cameras: cutting chips toward the camera, clearing hands to show they are empty, and spreading cards in specific patterns for verification. A dealer who receives surveillance write-ups for failing to follow these procedures faces disciplinary action."));
body.push(para("The severity of surveillance enforcement is documented extensively in the TruePokerDealer community. One former dealer reported receiving over 40 write-ups in a single review period for performing coin magic tricks with tip chips at the table. Surveillance could not track where the chips went during the sleight-of-hand movements and built a file documenting every instance. The write-ups covered \u2018taking a tip and not being able to determine where it went\u2019 \u2014 even though the chips were going into the tip box every time. The incident illustrates that surveillance prioritizes trackability above all else: if a chip movement cannot be followed frame-by-frame on camera, it is flagged regardless of intent. This principle is critical for robotic system design: every chip and card movement must be traceable on camera."));
body.push(para("A robotic system must integrate with existing surveillance infrastructure and may be able to provide even better camera angles and data feeds than human dealing allows. The system could provide direct digital audit logs to surveillance in addition to camera feeds, creating a dual-verification system that exceeds what is possible with human dealers."));

// ── 14. THE DEALER EXPERIENCE ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("14. The Human Dealer Experience: What Automation Replaces"));
body.push(para("Understanding what the job of dealing actually feels like is critical context for designing a robotic replacement that casino operators and the poker community will accept. This section is based on extensive interviews with active dealers and dealer supervisors."));

body.push(h2("14.1 Physical Toll"));
body.push(para("A standard dealing shift is 8 hours. Dealers work in \u2018downs\u2019 of approximately 30 minutes (dealing at one table), then rotate to a break or another table. In a typical 8-hour shift, a dealer may deal 10 to 12 tables with approximately 4 breaks totaling about 2 hours off the table. The actual dealing time is 5 to 6 hours of continuous repetitive motion."));
body.push(para("The most serious chronic injury is the ganglion cyst, a fluid-filled lump that develops on the wrist from repetitive strain. This is directly caused by incorrect pitch mechanics \u2014 specifically, pitching with wrist flick action instead of finger extension. Multiple dealers in the TruePokerDealer community have documented developing ganglion cysts that persist for years. One dealer developed a visible cyst after pitching with wrist action and had to be retrained to use the correct finger-extension technique. The cyst remained after retraining. This injury is almost entirely preventable with correct mechanics, which is why proper pitch training is considered a medical priority, not just a technique preference."));
body.push(para("Back and neck pain are the most universal complaints, particularly for shorter dealers. Casino poker tables are a standard size designed for average-height dealers. A dealer who is 5\u20194\u201D must reach significantly further across the table than a dealer who is 6\u20190\u201D, and that additional reach compounds over thousands of repetitions per shift. Players who place their bets close to themselves (rather than pushing them toward the center) force the dealer to reach further for every single bet, worsening the strain. Poker room chairs with inadequate back support (common in many rooms) compound the problem. Dealers report that working 5 or more consecutive days causes the most severe body issues, even when individual shifts are manageable."));
body.push(para("Shoulder strain develops from the repeated reaching motion across the table. The pitch itself, when done correctly, does not cause shoulder issues (it uses only the fingers). But gathering bets, pushing pots, and reaching for cards at the far seats creates cumulative shoulder fatigue over a career."));

body.push(h2("14.2 Compensation Structure"));
body.push(para("Poker dealers are primarily compensated through tips (tokes). Base hourly pay ranges from approximately $3/hour (in states that allow tip credit against minimum wage) to full state minimum wage (Washington state, for example, pays over $12/hour base). The job is structurally similar to restaurant serving: the base pay is a fraction of actual earnings, with tips making up the majority. All-in hourly earnings (base plus tips) average $25 to $40 per hour for a competent dealer in a mid-stakes game, with significant variance. A dealer working 3 to 4 days per week can take home $600 to $1,000+ after taxes, making it competitive with many other service industry jobs for the hours worked."));
body.push(para("Factors affecting tip income are well-documented in dealer interviews: (1) Dealing speed \u2014 more hands per hour means more pots won and more tip opportunities. Fast dealers consistently out-earn slow dealers. (2) Friendliness and personality \u2014 players tip more when they enjoy the dealer. However, friendliness creates vulnerability: dealers who open up their personality to players risk having that personality attacked by losing or angry players. Many dealers, especially women, adopt a deliberately neutral demeanor (\u2018shut up and deal\u2019) to protect themselves emotionally, knowingly sacrificing significant tip income. One experienced dealer estimated she would make \u2018twice as much money\u2019 if she were friendlier and more engaging, but chose not to because the emotional cost was too high. (3) Attractiveness \u2014 documented in dealer interviews as a real factor, particularly for female dealers. Flirtation can be used as a deliberate tip-maximizing strategy, but comes with the cost of unwanted attention and being hit on at the table. (4) Game stakes \u2014 higher stakes games generally produce larger tips. The difference between dealing a 1/3 game and a 5/10 game can be the difference between $25/hour and $50/hour in tips."));
body.push(para("The tip-dependent model creates significant income instability. A dealer\u2019s income can swing wildly based on table assignment, player composition, and luck (players tip more when they are winning). Chip runners (the staff who handle buy-ins and run chips to tables) are also tip-dependent, with a biggest-single-tip ceiling around $100-$175 from generous regulars, but average tips much lower. The entire compensation structure of casino poker operations depends on player generosity, which is a fragile foundation."));
body.push(para("Tax treatment of tips varies by state and has practical consequences beyond the paycheck. In states like Florida, tips are reported on taxes, which means they count as documented income for purposes like mortgage applications. In other states, tips may be partially unreported cash, which makes take-home pay higher but documented income lower \u2014 creating problems when dealers try to buy a house or car. Vacation time is another compensation gap: even full-time dealers with PTO are paid only their base rate during vacation, not their tip rate. A vacation that costs $2,000 in expenses also costs $1,500+ in lost tips, making vacations effectively double-priced for dealers."));
body.push(para("A hidden financial risk: dealers who were previously earning far less money before entering the industry suddenly have significantly more disposable income. Combined with constant exposure to gambling (sitting at a poker table for 40+ hours per week), many dealers develop gambling habits. The familiarity with gaming creates comfort that leads to experimentation, and the sliding scale of wagering can escalate quickly. This is more common with table games dealers (who are exposed to beatable-looking games like blackjack) but also occurs with poker dealers who start playing with the money they are earning."));

body.push(h2("14.3 Player Abuse and Table Culture"));
body.push(para("Dealers routinely experience verbal abuse from losing players. The standard negative behavior includes: aggressively folding cards, whispering under their breath, cursing, blaming the dealer for bad cards (\u2018I haven\u2019t seen a face card in an hour,\u2019 \u2018Are there any aces in this deck?\u2019), and general woe-is-me commentary. These complaints are constant and repetitive. Players learn these speech patterns from each other, creating a self-reinforcing culture of negativity at the table. On a typical 8-handed table, at least 5 players will be exhibiting some form of negative behavior at any given time."));
body.push(para("The recommended dealer response protocol is: take a few breaths, count down from 10 (or 5 if the pace of the game does not allow 10), remain calm and composed. Human beings naturally tend to mirror one another, so a levelheaded dealer will eventually bring a negative player back to baseline. The dealer should sincerely listen, try to understand the player\u2019s frustration, and offer a degree of sympathy. Most disgruntled players will calm down when they feel heard. In poker specifically (unlike table games where the dealer is the opponent), the dealer should never argue, disagree, or resist. Any resistance from a player should result in an immediate floor call. Confrontation in poker tends to spread (the game is inherently adversarial between players), so de-escalation by the dealer is critical."));
body.push(para("Female dealers face additional challenges. Being hit on at the table is described as \u2018horrible\u2019 and \u2018so awkward\u2019 by female dealers in the TruePokerDealer community \u2014 made worse by the fact that 7 or 8 other players are watching. Unwanted attention is sometimes accompanied by tips (\u2018if you\u2019re gonna hit on me, send the money\u2019), creating a transactional dynamic that is uncomfortable regardless of the financial benefit. Female dealers who are skilled and knowledgeable about the game report less bullying because they leave fewer openings for criticism, but the harassment does not disappear entirely. The poker room environment is described as \u2018just not a place that\u2019s pleasant for women\u2019 by both male and female industry professionals."));
body.push(para("Floor supervisor training for handling abuse is widely acknowledged as inadequate in the industry. Supervisors are typically \u2018thrown into it\u2019 without simulation training, shadowing, or structured preparation for confrontational situations. The result is that when a dealer calls the floor about an abusive player, the supervisor may not handle it effectively, sometimes even siding with the player. This failure of the support system is reported as more demoralizing than the abuse itself. One dealer described being brought to tears not by the abusive player but by the floor supervisor who failed to support her."));

body.push(h2("14.4 Break Procedures and the Push System"));
body.push(para("Dealers rotate through tables and breaks on a push system managed by supervisors. A typical push starts at a scheduled time: a relief dealer goes to the first table, waits for the current hand to complete, taps in, and the outgoing dealer moves to the next table. The push cascades through several tables before the last dealer goes on break. Breaks are nominally 30 minutes, but the push cascade can consume 5 to 10 minutes, leaving 20 to 25 minutes of actual break time."));
body.push(para("When going on break, the dealer should check in with a supervisor to confirm they are actually supposed to go to break. This seems unnecessary, but dealers occasionally go on break when they should be going to the next table, or when the supervisor intended a different rotation. A quick \u2018break, correct?\u2019 prevents misunderstandings and earns supervisor trust. During breaks, dealers should go directly to the break room and stay there. Lingering on the gaming floor during breaks is a major pet peeve of supervisors across the industry. Some supervisors will put a lingering dealer to work (opening a table, running a dead spread), which is within their authority. The break room is where the dealer should be, 100% of the time, unless a supervisor specifically asks them to remain on the floor."));
body.push(para("Seat cushions or mats: a practical detail that affects every dealer who follows you in the rotation. Dealers who sweat during their 30-minute down leave wet spots on the seat. This is universally considered disgusting and unprofessional. Most poker rooms allow dealers to bring a personal seat cushion (memory foam or similar) or a thin mat to place on the seat. If you are a person who sweats, investing in a seat cushion is not optional \u2014 it is a courtesy to every dealer in the rotation behind you."));

body.push(h2("14.5 The EO (Early Out) List"));
body.push(para("Poker rooms have variable table counts throughout the day, meaning the number of dealers needed fluctuates constantly. The EO (early out) list allows dealers to volunteer to go home early when the room does not need them. If tables are closing and the room has more dealers than games, the supervisor checks the EO list and sends home volunteers first. This is a useful operational tool \u2014 supervisors appreciate it because it simplifies the difficult decision of who to send home."));
body.push(para("However, signing the EO list regularly creates a reputation problem. Supervisors and shift managers notice dealers who always want to leave early, and it is interpreted as not wanting to be there. This perception affects promotion decisions (\u2018they\u2019re on the EO list all the time\u2019 has killed promotion conversations), shift preferences, and overall standing in the room. Additionally, being on the EO list creates no entitlement: a dealer who signs up cannot expect to leave by a specific time. There is no guaranteed end time for a dealer shift \u2014 this is a fundamental reality of the casino industry that new dealers must accept before starting. Making plans contingent on getting an early out is a recipe for frustration and resentment."));

body.push(h2("14.6 Companywide Meetings"));
body.push(para("Every casino holds periodic companywide meetings that all employees are expected to attend. These meetings may occur on a dealer\u2019s day off and are typically compensated at minimum wage (not tip rate). The meetings cover operational updates, policy changes, safety briefings, and team-building. Dealers who view these meetings as \u2018not worth their time\u2019 or who skip them are noted by management. Attendance at companywide meetings is treated as a basic indicator of whether the dealer is a cooperative team member or a problem. This expectation exists at every casino, at every level from dealer to supervisor. Accepting it before starting the job prevents the resentment that many dealers develop when they first encounter it."));

body.push(h2("14.7 Representing the Casino"));
body.push(para("Dealers represent the casino at all times \u2014 not just while on the clock. Casino employees are expected to maintain a professional reputation both inside and outside the property. This extends to behavior at competing properties: a dealer who plays poker at another room and is rude to staff or players there can be disciplined at their own casino. The industry is small, and information travels fast. Everything a dealer does in the poker ecosystem gets back to the poker director eventually."));
body.push(para("The most damaging behavior is telling a player to go play somewhere else. One dealer observed at a property told a difficult player, \u2018If you like how they do it at [competing room], go play there.\u2019 That dealer received a severe write-up. The casino\u2019s business depends on every player returning, and sending them to a competitor is directly contrary to the dealer\u2019s job. The correct response to any player complaint about how the room operates is empathy and, if necessary, a floor call \u2014 never a suggestion to play elsewhere."));
body.push(para("Player relationships represent an enormous untapped opportunity for dealers. Every table seats 6 to 10 people for 30 minutes, and many of those people are professionals, business owners, and high-net-worth individuals. Dealers who build genuine relationships with players have been invited to deal private games (where tips can exceed $2,000 for an 8-hour session), offered better-paying jobs by player connections, and created networking opportunities that changed their careers. The dealing job is not just a paycheck \u2014 it is an unparalleled networking platform for anyone who recognizes it as such."));

body.push(h2("14.8 The Players Card as a Revenue Tool"));
body.push(para("The player\u2019s card (the casino\u2019s loyalty/rewards card) is one of the most important business tools in the poker room, yet dealers routinely dismiss it. Every player who carries a player\u2019s card is being continuously advertised to: every time they open their wallet, see the card in their car, or notice it on their kitchen counter, they think about coming back to play. More returning players means more tables, more tables means more hours for dealers, and more hours means more income."));
body.push(para("Most poker rooms offer comps (typically $0.40 to $3.00 per hour of play) to players who use their cards. Many players would happily accept a card if the process were frictionless, but they do not want to do the work of going to the cashier. The dealer who says \u2018Give me your ID, I\u2019ll get you one \u2014 no problem\u2019 captures players that the dealer who says \u2018Do you want a player\u2019s card?\u2019 (which invites a \u2018no\u2019) would lose. If every dealer in a room captures 10 additional player card signups per month, the cumulative effect on room traffic is measurable. Rooms that have tracked this report that card-carrying players return at significantly higher rates than cardless players, even when the comp rate is modest."));

body.push(h2("14.9 What This Means for Robotic Deployment"));
body.push(para("A robotic dealer eliminates the physical injuries, income instability, and emotional abuse that characterize the human dealing experience. This is not just a cost savings argument for casino operators. It is a labor conditions argument. The poker community\u2019s emotional attachment to human dealers is genuine, but it coexists with a system that objectively harms the people it depends on. A robotic system that handles the mechanical dealing while preserving elite human dealers for premium and broadcast events represents an improvement for everyone: faster and more accurate games for players, cost savings for operators, and a transition to better roles for the best human dealers."));
body.push(para("The EO list, push rotation, break management, attendance tracking, and companywide meeting coordination all disappear when dealing is automated. Dead spreads are replaced by standby mode. Seat cushion hygiene is irrelevant. The casino\u2019s representation concerns shift from managing hundreds of individual dealer personalities to maintaining a single system\u2019s brand identity. Player card integration becomes automatic \u2014 a robotic system can prompt card use, track comps per hour digitally, and eliminate the friction that causes players to play without their cards."));

// ── 15. DEALER CAREER AND PROFESSIONAL DEVELOPMENT ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("15. Dealer Career and Professional Development"));
body.push(para("This section covers the career lifecycle of a poker dealer: how to get hired, how to succeed, and the professional development path from new hire to veteran. While a robotic dealing system eliminates the need for human dealers in the mechanical role, understanding the human career path provides essential context for the transition strategy and for designing systems that casino operators \u2014 many of whom came up through the dealing ranks \u2014 will trust and accept."));

body.push(h2("15.1 The Interview: Five Critical Questions"));
body.push(para("Casino dealer interviews follow a relatively predictable format. Even when these exact questions are not asked, communicating the answers proactively gives the interviewer confidence in the candidate. The five questions below represent the core concerns of every poker room hiring manager."));
body.push(h3("Question 1: What times can you work?"));
body.push(para("The casino industry exists outside normal business hours. Players come after work, on weekends, and on holidays. Dealers must be available for nights, overnights, weekends, and every major holiday without exception. Anyone who is not willing to work these hours should not enter the industry. New dealers will not get day shifts \u2014 those go to full-time veterans with years of seniority. The correct answer communicates flexibility, dedication, and awareness that the casino industry is not 9-to-5. Bonus: telling the interviewer \u2018you can give me 30 minutes notice and I\u2019ll be here\u2019 (if true) is an enormous differentiator, because last-minute staffing is one of the biggest operational headaches in every poker room."));
body.push(h3("Question 2: What relevant work experience do you have?"));
body.push(para("The experience hierarchy from most valuable to least: casino dealer or casino employee of any type, dealer school graduate, food and beverage or hospitality combined with dealer training, general customer-focused experience, and poker player. Poker playing experience is a double-edged sword: familiarity with game procedures is helpful, but poker players who become dealers sometimes carry an ego that creates friction with supervisors and players. The candidate should frame their experience in terms of what it does for the employer, not what it means to them. \u2018I\u2019ve been observing dealers for years and I understand the difference between good and bad habits\u2019 is far better than \u2018I\u2019m a really good poker player.\u2019"));
body.push(h3("Question 3: What makes a good dealer?"));
body.push(para("Three answers matter: teamwork, guest service, and positivity. Teamwork is the most important \u2014 a dealer who makes everyone else\u2019s job easier is worth more than a technically perfect dealer who creates interpersonal friction. Guest service means accuracy, attentiveness, and creating positive experiences. Positivity means maintaining composure and energy in a hostile environment where players will test the dealer\u2019s patience for hours. Being solutions-oriented (rather than rules-oriented) is a valuable way to present this quality. The candidate should speak from genuine personal values, not rehearsed talking points \u2014 interviewers can tell the difference."));
body.push(h3("Question 4: Where do you see yourself in 5 years?"));
body.push(para("The trap: appearing arrogant or short-term. \u2018I\u2019ll be running this room\u2019 communicates ego. \u2018I\u2019ll probably have moved on\u2019 communicates that the casino is a stepping stone. The ideal answer communicates patience, dedication, and a desire to earn advancement through demonstrated competence. \u2018I want to be so good at this job that supervisors come and ask me if I\u2019d be interested in a promotion\u2019 is the right tone. Hiring managers also value candidates who simply want to be excellent, long-term dealers \u2014 a full-time dealer who stays for 30 years is a massive win for the casino because that seat is never vacant."));
body.push(h3("Question 5: How would you deal with a difficult customer?"));
body.push(para("The answer framework: keep the guest happy, be solutions-focused, and follow company policy. In poker specifically, the dealer must balance the fact that making one player happy often makes eight others unhappy (the game is adversarial). The key differentiator is communicating that the dealer will call the floor when necessary \u2014 not aggressively, but calmly and respectfully. Never trying to solve everything alone, never arguing, never taking sides. The combination of \u2018keep guests happy, find solutions, follow policy, call the floor when needed\u2019 is the answer that every experienced poker room manager wants to hear."));

body.push(h2("15.2 The Audition"));
body.push(para("Many poker rooms evaluate candidates through a dealing audition rather than (or in addition to) a traditional interview. In a typical audition, 6 to 15 candidates arrive together. One candidate sits in the dealer\u2019s chair while the others act as players. The audition evaluator observes the candidate\u2019s shuffle, pitch, game management, chip handling, and overall demeanor. While one candidate deals, the evaluator is also observing how the other candidates behave as players \u2014 their sportsmanship, attitude, and interaction style are all being assessed."));
body.push(para("The audition is where practice pays off. Everything taught in dealer school or online training (shuffle sequence, pitch mechanics, chip cutting, pot management) must be muscle memory before the audition. No amount of knowledge compensates for mechanical incompetence. The evaluator is looking for: clean shuffle with proper wash, confident pitch that lands cards in front of players, accurate chip handling, correct game procedure, and \u2014 critically \u2014 the ability to manage the table while dealing. A candidate who deals perfectly but cannot follow the action, announce bets, or track player positions will not be hired."));

body.push(h2("15.3 Constant Observation"));
body.push(para("After being hired, the dealer is observed and evaluated every second of every shift. This is not an exaggeration. The way the dealer interacts with other team members, their behavior in the break room, their attitude during dead spreads, their punctuality, their appearance, and their table presence are all continuously assessed. Every poker room has people who are closer to management than other dealers realize \u2014 information about dealer behavior travels through informal channels to the poker director. Everything gets back to the director. This is not a paranoid worldview; it is a structural reality of a small, insular work environment where supervisors and dealers spend 8+ hours together daily."));
body.push(para("The practical implication: treat every interaction as if the poker director is watching, because functionally, they are. A dealer who gossips in the break room, complains about table assignments, creates conflict with other dealers, or exhibits any other unprofessional behavior is building a file against themselves \u2014 even if no formal write-up is generated. When opportunities for advancement arise, these informal assessments weigh heavily."));

body.push(h2("15.4 Tribal and Indian Casino Considerations"));
body.push(para("Tribal casinos operate under different regulatory and cultural frameworks than commercial casinos. Some tribes are small with many tribal members working in the casino; others are large enterprises (like the Seminole tribe\u2019s Hard Rock properties) where few tribal members work directly in operations. Dealers at tribal casinos should expect to encounter and respect tribal culture during orientation and daily operations. Sovereign Nation status means different employment laws may apply \u2014 the Seminole properties, for instance, have a formal trial process before termination where outside parties evaluate whether the firing is justified, rather than at-will employment."));
body.push(para("Promotion policies at tribal casinos universally give preference to tribal members (typically 1/8 or more tribal heritage). This is not discrimination \u2014 it is a fundamental condition of the tribal gaming compact that allows the casino to operate. Dealers who do not understand or accept this should not work at tribal properties. The cultural experience can be deeply rewarding: exposure to tribal traditions, ceremonies, and ways of life that most people never encounter. But it requires cultural sensitivity and an absence of entitlement."));

body.push(h2("15.5 Smoking Policies"));
body.push(para("Almost the entire poker industry prohibits smoking in the poker room or designated poker area. This is a significant advantage over table games floors, where smoking may be permitted (especially in tribal casinos where state clean-air laws do not apply). Some casinos have the poker area as part of the general casino floor, where smoke from adjacent areas can drift in, but the poker tables themselves are smoke-free. For dealers considering whether to work in poker or table games, the smoke-free poker environment is a meaningful quality-of-life factor over a multi-year career."));

body.push(boldPara("Robotic Requirement: ", "The career infrastructure described in this section \u2014 hiring, training, evaluation, promotion, cultural navigation \u2014 is eliminated by robotic dealing. A robotic system requires no interviews, no auditions, no attendance management, no break scheduling, no EO lists, no companywide meetings, no moonlighting policies, and no tribal preference navigation. The system is deployed, configured, and maintained. The human career path becomes relevant only for floor staff, supervisors, and the technicians who maintain the robotic systems. Understanding this career path remains important for two reasons: (1) the decision-makers who will approve robotic deployment are people who went through this career path themselves, and (2) the transition plan must account for the human impact on an existing workforce of dealers who depend on this career for their livelihood."));

// ── APPENDIX ──
body.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
body.push(h1("Appendix A: Complete Hand Cycle Checklist"));
body.push(para("This appendix provides the complete sequence of dealer actions for a single hand of No-Limit Texas Hold\u2019em, from shuffle to pot push. Each line item represents a discrete action that a robotic system must be capable of executing."));

